\begin{abstract}
We prove tight upper and lower bounds of $\Theta\left(\tfrac{1}{\epsilon} \left(\sqrt{2^k
\log\binom{n}{k}} + \log\binom{n}{k}\right)\right)$ on the number of samples required for 
distribution-free $k$-junta testing. This is the first tight bound for testing a natural class of
Boolean functions in the distribution-free sample-based model. Our bounds also hold for the feature selection problem, showing that a junta tester must learn the set of relevant
variables.  For tolerant junta testing, we prove a sample lower bound of $\Omega(2^{(1-o(1))k} + \log\binom{n}{k})$ showing that, unlike standard testing, there is no large gap
between tolerant testing and learning.

\end{abstract}

