\begin{abstract}
Quadratization refers to a transformation of an arbitrary system of polynomial ordinary differential equations to a system with at most quadratic right-hand side.  
Such a transformation unveils new variables and model structures that facilitate model analysis, simulation, and control and offer a convenient parameterization for data-driven approaches. 
Quadratization techniques have found applications in diverse fields, including systems theory, fluid mechanics, chemical reaction modeling, and mathematical analysis.

In this study, we focus on quadratizations that preserve the stability properties of the original model, specifically dissipativity at given equilibria. 
This preservation is desirable in many applications of quadratization including reachability analysis and synthetic biology. 
We establish the existence of dissipativity-preserving quadratizations, develop an algorithm for their computation, and demonstrate it in several case studies.

\keywords{differential equations  \and quadratization \and stability \and variable transformation}
\end{abstract}
