\subsection{Control Approaches}
Two different control approaches are described and assessed in the following: 
\label{sec:control-approach}
\textbf{Linear programming (LP)}: 
%The \gls{lp} approach formulates the objective function and main constraints for optimal battery scheduling and power split control. 
This method adopts a rolling horizon-based deterministic linear programming technique to schedule and operate a \gls{bess}. It relies on forecasts of both load demand and PV generation, which may be based on either perfect foresight or persistence-based predictions~\cite{Moshovel.2015}. The optimization problem is formulated as a multi-objective framework, with the primary objective of minimizing the total operational cost, as expressed in Equation \eqref{eq:LP_obj}. In achieving this, the formulation simultaneously addresses secondary objectives, namely the balancing of the \gls{soc} and the uniformity of temperature $\tau$ across the battery strings. These secondary goals are critical for enhancing the performance, extending the lifespan, and ensuring the safety of the \gls{bess}. To ensure comparability among the different objectives, the coefficients ($x$,$y$,$z$) in the objective function are selected such that each individual objective is normalized. %\textcolor{red}{The optimization model assumes fixed charge/discharge efficiencies for batteries and generates optimal power set-points $p^{B[m]}$ for each battery module. Objective weights....} %These set-points are computed such that they balance load supply while maintaining \gls{soc} equity and thermal uniformity. In addition to economic objectives, the LP model incorporates a comprehensive set of physical and thermal constraints to ensure safe operation. These include \gls{soc} limits, which ensure each battery pack operates within its allowable \gls{soc} range to prevent overcharging or deep discharging~\cite{}, and temperature constraints, which limit the operating temperature to avoid thermal runaway and degradation, based on manufacturer specifications and thermal safety thresholds~\cite{}.


\begin{equation}
\label{eq:LP_obj}
\begin{aligned}
Objective = \min \Bigg(\sum_{t \in T}\left(x\cdot cost_t + y \cdot \Delta soc_t + z \cdot\Delta \tau_t\right) \Bigg)
\end{aligned}
\end{equation}

Subject to, for all $t \in T$ and $m \in M$:

\begin{equation}
p_t^{G,buy},\ p_t^{G,sell} \geq 0
\end{equation}

\begin{equation}
