\subsection{Metrics}
To assess the effectiveness of the controllers under the defined simulation scenarios, we employ a set of quantitative performance metrics. These include economic savings, balancing metrics for SOC and temperature, and round-trip efficiency ($\eta$). Each metric captures a different aspect of the energy management system's behavior, enabling a comprehensive comparison across controller types and scenarios.
\begin{itemize}
    \item \textbf{Savings~(€):}
    \textcolor{red}{Sheng to check} This measures the total cost reduction achieved by the controller. All controllers operate under the same pricing and load conditions, with both perfect and persistent forecasts applied. This ensures a fair comparison of how effectively each controller minimizes energy costs through intelligent scheduling and battery usage. Greater savings indicate better optimization of energy purchases and storage utilization in response to dynamic pricing signals.
    \item \textbf{\(\Delta\)SOC}: This metric quantifies the deviation of individual battery strings’ SOC from the system-wide mean SOC over time. At each time step $t$, the mean SOC across all battery strings is calculated as shown in Eq.~\ref{eq:soc_objective}\cite{Tanjavooru.2025}

    \item \textbf{\(\Delta\)\scalebox{1.5}{$\tau$}}: 
    This metric provides insight into thermal imbalances across the \gls{bess} as defined in Eq.~\ref{eq:t_objective}. These are critical in the context of battery degradation. A higher $\Delta$\scalebox{1.5}{$\tau$} indicates greater thermal inconsistency, potentially leading to accelerated and uneven aging, or even thermal runaway in the affected strings \cite{}.

    \item \textbf{Efficiency ($\eta$):} Energy or Power ?? \textcolor{orange}{energy?} Round-trip efficiency captures the energy efficiency of the battery system as governed by each controller. It is defined as the proportion of useful energy output from the battery system relative to the energy input, accounting for all conversion and thermal losses:
\end{itemize}
