\begin{abstract}
%In the field of robotics, determining the navigable areas for a robot is crucial. This study addresses the traversability of outdoor terrains by robots. Our novel proposal is a straightforward yet effective concept: a robot can navigate an area if it has previously navigated that area or one very similar to it. Our method involves mapping elevations and textures onto a multi-layered map, which will be used to navigate autonomously. During the training phase, the robot learns which parts of the environment are traversable, using the teleoperated robot. In the deployment phase, the robot identifies similar learned areas as traversable using an encoder-decoder neural network. It processes the features from the multi-layer map and gets distances between the encoder-decoder output. This method demands minimal training effort and facilitates quick learning. We tested our approach on real outdoor robots, both wheeled and legged, with successful results in determining which areas are traversable. The system expands traversable zones in real-time as the robot encounters and crosses new types of terrain. These new types of zones can be defined based on factors such as surface texture (color) or the roughness and inclination of the terrain, allowing the robot to adapt dynamically to varying environmental conditions.

Accurate traversability estimation is essential for safe and effective navigation of outdoor robots operating in complex environments. This paper introduces a novel experience-based method that allows robots to autonomously learn which terrains are traversable based on prior navigation experience, without relying on extensive pre-labeled datasets. The approach integrates elevation and texture data into multi-layered grid maps, which are processed using a variational autoencoder (VAE) trained on a generic texture dataset. During an initial teleoperated phase, the robot collects sensory data while moving around the environment. These experiences are encoded into compact feature vectors and clustered using the BIRCH algorithm to represent traversable terrain areas efficiently. In deployment, the robot compares new terrain patches to its learned feature clusters to assess traversability in real time. The proposed method does not require training with data from the targeted scenarios, generalizes across diverse surfaces and platforms, and dynamically adapts as new terrains are encountered. Extensive evaluations on both synthetic benchmarks and real-world scenarios with wheeled and legged robots demonstrate its effectiveness, robustness, and superior adaptability compared to state-of-the-art approaches.
\end{abstract}

