\begin{abstract}
We are interested in the generalised word problem (aka subgroup membership problem) for stabiliser subgroups of groups acting on rooted $d$-regular trees. Stabilisers of infinite rays in the tree are not finitely generated in general, and so the problem is not even well posed unless the infinite ray has a finite description, for example, if the ray is eventually periodic as an infinite word over the alphabet with $d$ letters. We show that for bounded automata groups, the membership problem for such subgroups is solvable by proving that it forms an ET0L language that is constructable.
Exploiting this, we give a recursive formula for the associated generating function.
We also show that, in general, the membership problem for the stabiliser of an infinite ray in a bounded automata group cannot be described using context-free languages.
\end{abstract}
\section{Introduction}\label{sec:introduction}

In an influential paper from 1911 \cite{dehn1911}, Max Dehn formulated three decision problems for finitely generated groups, the most famous of which being the \emph{word problem}.
Given a finitely generated group $G$ with finite symmetric generating set $X$, the word problem asks if we can decide, given a word in the free monoid $w\in X^*$, whether its natural projection to the group $\overline{w}$ is the trivial element (i.e.~belongs to the trivial subgroup).
It is then natural to extend this to the so-called \emph{generalised word problem} which asks, given finitely many elements $g_1,g_2,...,g_k\in G$ and a word $w\in X^*$, whether $\overline{w}$ belongs to the subgroup $ \left\langle g_1,g_2,...,g_k \right\rangle$.

For an arbitrary subgroup $H\leq G$, denote by $\WP(G,X,H)$ the set of all words $w\in X^*$ for which $\overline{w}$ is an element of the subgroup $H$.
We can then ask for which classes of groups and subgroups the membership to $\WP(G,X,H)$ is computationally decidable.
Let us also mention a variant called the \emph{uniform generalised word problem} which asks for an algorithm which takes as input both a word $w\in X^*$ and a description of a subgroup $H\leq G$, and requires that this algorithm determines $\overline{w}$'s membership in $H$. This is also known as the \emph{subgroup membership problem}. The literature on the subgroup membership problem has mainly been concerned with the case where $H$ is given by a finite generating set.

In this paper, we consider the subgroup membership problem in the class of bounded automata groups (see \cref{def:bounded-automata-automorphism}). Automata groups are defined by their action on an infinite regular rooted tree $\mathcal{T}_d$, $d\geq 2$ which we identify with the monoid of words in the alphabet $C=\{c_1,...,c_d\}$.
This class of groups includes many examples with exotic properties, such as groups of intermediate growth, infinite torsion groups, non-elementary amenable groups and more \cite{Bondarenko2007thesis,Nekrashevych2005book}.
Among their subgroups, a special role is played by point stabilisers for the action of the group on $\mathcal{T}_d$ and on its boundary $\partial\mathcal{T}_d$ formed by the infinite rays of the rooted tree (i.e., one-sided infinite words in the alphabet $C$). We denote by $\Stab(\eta)$ the stabiliser of the infinite ray $\eta = c_{i_1} c_{i_2} c_{i_3} \cdots \in C^\omega$, and we have \[\Stab(\eta) = \bigcap_{k=1}^\infty \Stab(c_{i_1} c_{i_2}\cdots c_{i_k})\] where each $\Stab(c_{i_1} \cdots c_{i_k})$ is the stabiliser in $G$ of the vertex of the tree corresponding to the word $c_{i_1}c_{i_2}\cdots c_{i_k}$.

The stabilisers of the tree vertices are subgroups of finite index and so the subgroup membership problem to them is decidable. On the other hand, the stabilisers of infinite rays are in general not even finitely generated, so the question of decidability of the membership problem in such a subgroup is not well-posed, as one would need to describe the subgroup with a finite amount of information. This can be done, however, if the infinite ray corresponds to an eventually periodic word in $C$, and it is the subgroup membership problem to stabilisers of such rays that we consider in this paper. To the best of our knowledge, this is the first study of the subgroup membership problem into subgroups which are not finitely generated.

Another interesting aspect of the subgroup membership problem is to describe the formal language $\WP(G,X,H)$ for given $G, X$ and $H$. Although this has been studied extensively for the case when $H$ is trivial (the word problem), practically nothing is known for the case of non-trivial $H$. We can think about these two cases respectively as of the word problem on the Cayley graph $(G,X)$ and on the Schreier graph $(G,X,H)$, where the graphs are labelled and oriented, and the language is formed by the words read along the closed loops based at the vertex representing the identity element in the Cayley graph (respectively the coset $e \cdot H$ in the Schreier graph).

For a subgroup $H$ of finite index, such a language will always be regular, since the Schreier graph is then a finite-state automaton which recognises membership into the subgroup.
In \cref{thm:main}, we show that the language of all words belonging to the stabiliser of an eventually periodic ray forms an ET0L language.
Let us note here that it is conjectured (see Conjecture~8.1 in~\cite{ciobanu2018}) that a group has an ET0L word problem if and only if it is virtually free, thus for ET0L languages it is potentially more interesting to consider the generalised word problem rather than the word problem.
Moreover, we note here that to the authors' knowledge, this is the first time that the subgroup membership problem has been considered from a formal language perspective.

Introduced by Rozenberg in 1973~\cite{Rozenberg1973}, the class of ET0L languages has recently come into prominence within the field of geometric group theory.
In particular, ET0L systems have been used to characterise the set of solutions to equations over certain classes of groups (see~\cite{diekert2017,ciobanu2019,evetts2020,diekert2001}), to characterise the coword problem of bounded automata groups~\cite{Bishop2019}, and some other problems~\cite{ciobanu2018}.

Our main result in this paper is the following theorem.

\begin{restatable*}{theorem}{TheoremMain}\label{thm:main}
Let $G \leq \AutTd$ be a finitely generated bounded automata group acting on the tree $\mathcal T_d  = C\star$.
For each infinite ray of the form $\eta = a b^\omega$  with $a,b \in C\star$, the languages $\WP(G,X,\Stab(\eta))$ and its set complement $X^*\setminus \WP(G,X,\Stab(\eta))$ are both ET0L languages of the form defined in \cref{def:unambiguous-et0l}.
The ET0L grammars of these two languages are effectively constructable from the above description of the ray $\eta$.
\end{restatable*}

The membership to a fixed ET0L language is known to be decidable in space complexity $O(n)$ and time complexity $O(n^2)$ where $n$ is the length of the input word (see Lemma~2.1 in~\cite{leeuwen1976}).
Hence, our theorem immediately implies that the generalised word problem is solvable in this case.
After some slight modifications to this proof, we show in \cref{lem:ET0L-membership-uniformly-decidable} that membership to ET0L languages is uniformly decidable, and thus
we have the following corollary to \cref{thm:main}.

\begin{restatable*}{corollary}{TheoremMainCor}\label{cor:solvability}
For the class of finitely generated bounded automata groups, the uniform generalised word problem is solvable for the class of subgroups $\Stab(\eta)$ where $\eta$ is an eventually periodic ray.
That is, for each bounded automata groups, there exists an algorithm which can take as input a word and eventually periodic ray, and decides $w$'s membership to $\WP(G,X,\Stab(\eta))$.
\end{restatable*}

In our proof of \cref{thm:main}, we show that the ET0L grammar we derive can be described by a special subclass of ET0L grammars, as described in \cref{def:unambiguous-et0l}.
In the following theorem, we present a recurrence relation for the generating functions of such ET0L grammars.

\begin{restatable*}{theorem}{TheoremGFun}\label{thm:gfun}
Let $L\subseteq \Sigma\star$ be an \IfRestatedTF{ET0L language as in \cref{def:unambiguous-et0l}.}{unambiguous limiting ET0L language.}
Then, the generating function of $L$ can be written as
\[
    f(z) = g(r_1(z), r_2(z),...,r_k(z))
\]
where each $r_i(z)\in \mathbb{N}[[z]]$ is a rational function, and $g(x_1,x_2,...,x_k)$ is a formal power series defined as
\[
    g(x_1,x_2,...,x_k)
    =
    \lim_{m\to\infty} g_m(x_1,x_2,...,x_k)
\]
where $g_0(x_1,x_2,...,x_k)\in \mathbb N[[x_1,...,x_k]]$ is a rational power series, and
\begin{multline*}
    g_{m+1}(x_1,x_2,...,x_k) = g_{m}(q_1(x_1,x_2,...,x_k),q_2(x_1,x_2,...,x_k),
    \\...,q_k(x_1,x_2,...,x_k))
\end{multline*}
for each $m \geq 0$ where each $q_i(x_1,...,x_k) \in \mathbb{N}[[x_1,...,x_k]]$ is a rational series depending on $g_m$. In the above $k$ is a constant that depends on the grammar.
\end{restatable*}

Let us now introduce a useful tool for working with automata groups.
Let $\eta\in \partial \mathcal{T}_d$ be a point in the boundary of the infinite rooted tree on which an automata group $G = \left\langle X \right\rangle$ acts.
Denote by $\Gamma_\eta$ the \emph{Schreier graph of $\Stab(\eta)$} which is formed by the vertex set $\eta\cdot G = \{\eta\cdot g \mid g\in G\}$ with a labelled edge $\zeta\to^x (\zeta\acts x)$ for each vertex $\zeta$ and each generator $x\in X$.
We then see that \cref{thm:gfun} can be used to calculate the generating functions of closed walks on such Schreier graphs.
Indeed, \cref{thm:gfun} allows us to find a recurrence for the generating function $f(z)$ of the language $\mathrm{WP}(G,X,\mathrm{Stab}(\eta))$, as in \cref{thm:main}, which exactly describes all paths in $\Gamma_\eta$ which begin and end at the vertex $\eta$.
From this, we may find the Green function for the simple random walk on $\Gamma_\eta$ starting at $\eta$ as
\[
    G(z)
    =
    f(z/|X|)
\]
where $|X|$ is the size of the generating set of $G$. Recall that the Green function for a random walk is defined as
\[
    G(z)
    =
    \sum_{n=0}^\infty p^{(n)}(\eta,\eta)\, z^n
\]
where $p^{(n)} (\eta,\eta)$ is the probability of a random walk which starts at $\eta$, ending up back at $\eta$ after exactly $n$ steps.
We note here that \cref{thm:gfun} can be extended to also apply to arbitrary weighted random walks.
Information about the Green functions and their complexity is also valuable in the study of spectra of Schreier graphs (see for example \cite{Mohar1989}).

A common question when dealing with classes of formal languages is to ask where in the Chomsky hierarchy they live.
It is well known that the class of ET0L languages lies strictly between the families of context-free and context-sensitive languages (see Theorem~19 of \cite{Rozenberg1973}).
In \cref{sec:not-CF}, we provide arguments based on ends of Schreier graphs which give obstructions to the language associated to them being context-free.
In particular, we show that any stabiliser of an infinite ray $\eta$ in the first Grigorchuk group and other key examples gives a non-context-free language $\WP(G,X,\Stab(\eta))$. This gives a negative answer to the question of whether \cref{thm:main} can be improved to the class of context-free languages.

As a final remark, we note that the word problem for $G=\left\langle X \right\rangle$ can be written as the infinite intersection of all languages $\mathrm{WP}(G,X,\Stab(\eta))$ where $\eta$ is an eventually periodic ray.
This is clear since each vertex $v\in \mathcal T_d$ can be written as a prefix of an eventually periodic ray $vc_1^\omega$ where any action that stabilises $vc_1^\omega$ also stabilises $v$.
Hence, the words in such an intersection stabilises every vertex.
Motivated by this, we ask when the word problem can be written as a finite intersection of ET0L languages.

\Cref{sec:bounded-automata-groups,sec:et0l-language} are devoted to a general introduction on the main topic regarding bounded automata groups and ET0L languages, but also to provide useful lemmas and definitions needed in the rest of the paper. The proof of \cref{thm:main} is entirely contained in \cref{sec:main}, while \cref{sec:not-CF} gives criteria that can be used to find non-context-free language of stabilisers.
Lastly, \cref{sec:future} contains a list of open questions and topics for future research.

\section{Bounded Automata Groups}\label{sec:bounded-automata-groups}

As in the introduction, we write $\Td$ for a $d$-regular rooted tree with vertex set given by the words $C^*$ where $C = \{c_1,c_2,...,c_d\}$.
That is, the root of the tree is labelled by the empty word $\varepsilon\in C^*$, and each vertex $v\in C^*$ has exactly $d$ children given by $v c_1$, $v c_2$, ..., $v c_d$.
See \cref{fig:tree-vertex-labelling} for a depiction of this tree.

\begin{figure}[H]
	\centering
	\includegraphics{figure/labelledTree}
	\caption{A labelling of the vertices of $\Td$.}
	\label{fig:tree-vertex-labelling}
\end{figure}

We then write $\AutTd$ for the group of graph automorphisms of $\Td$.
We notice that every automorphism $\alpha \in \AutTd$ fixes the root and each level of the tree (i.e.~sends vertices to vertices of the same level).
Moreover, we see that $\AutTd = \AutTd\wr\mathrm{Sym}(C)$ where $\mathrm{Sym}(C)$ is the symmetric group on the set $C$.
That is, each automorphism $\alpha\in \AutTd$ can be uniquely written in the form $\alpha = (\alpha'_1, \alpha'_2,...,\alpha'_d)\cdot s$ where each $\alpha'_i\in \AutTd$ is an automorphism of the subtree rooted at $c_i$, and $s\in \mathrm{Sym}(C)$ is a permutation of the first-level subtrees.
Given such a decomposition $\alpha$, we write $\alpha\at{c_i} = \alpha'_i$ for the \emph{restriction of $\alpha$ to $c_i$}.
We then define the \emph{restriction} $\alpha \at{v}$ for each $\alpha\in \AutTd$ and $v=v_1 v_2 \cdots v_m \in C\star$ recursively as
\[
	\alpha\at{v}
	=
	\left(\alpha\at{v_1}\right)\at{v_2 v_3 \cdots v_m}.
\]
That is, $\alpha\at v$ is the action that the element $\alpha$ has on the subtree rooted at $v$.

In this paper, we are only interested in actions on the tree $\Td$ by \emph{bounded automaton automorphisms} which we define as follows.

\begin{definition}\label{def:bounded-automata-automorphism}
We say that $\alpha \in \AutTd$ is an \emph{automaton automorphism} if there exists a finite set $A_\alpha\subset \AutTd$ such that $\alpha \at v \in A_\alpha$ for each $v\in C^*$.
The set of all automaton automorphisms forms a group $\mathrm{AAut}(\Td)$.
A group $G\leq\AutTd$ is then called an \emph{automata group} if $G\leq \mathrm{AAut}(\Td)$.

Moreover, we say that an automorphism $\alpha \in \AutTd$ is \emph{bounded}~\cite{sidki2000} if there exists some constant $N_\alpha \in \N$ such that
\[
    \#\{
        v\in C\star
    \mid
        \alpha\at{v} \neq 1
        \text{ and }
        |v|=k
    \}
\]
is bounded uniformly in $k$.
The set of all bounded automaton automorphisms forms a group which we denote as $\BTd < \mathrm{AAut}(\Td)$.
A group $G$ is then called a \emph{bounded automata group} if it can be written as $G\leq \BTd$.
\end{definition}

In the literature, the class of automaton automorphisms is usually introduced by first defining a computational model known as a finite-state automaton, for example, see Definition~1.3.1 in~\cite{Nekrashevych2005book}.
We note here that our definition is identical, in particular, a finite set of states for an automorphism $\alpha$ is given by the set $A_\alpha$ as in \cref{def:bounded-automata-automorphism}.
Moreover, when the class of bounded automata groups is considered in the literature, it is often the case that such groups are also assumed to be \emph{self-similar} (also known as \emph{state-closed}).
We note here that we do not require our bounded automata groups to be self-similar.

The term `automata' (i.e.~the plural of `automaton') is used in the above definition since the generating set of such groups may contain several automaton automorphisms.

Notice that it follows from the above definition that given an automaton automorphism $x\in \AutTd$ and a vertex $v\in C^*$ in the tree $\Td$, the action $v\cdot x$ is computable.
In particular, if we are given the values $c_{\alpha,c} = c\cdot \alpha \in C$ and $\alpha_c = \alpha\at{c} \in A_x$ for each $\alpha\in A_x$ and $c\in C$, then we can calculate the action $v\cdot x$ for any $v = v_1 v_2 \cdots v_{k}$ as $v\cdot x = c_{\alpha,v_1}\, ((v_1 v_2 \cdots v_k)\cdot x_{v_1})$.
Notice that storing the values $c_{\alpha,c}$ and $\alpha_{c}$ only requires a finite amount of space, and thus the action is computable.

In~\cite{sidki2000}, Sidki considered two classes of bounded automaton automorphisms, known as the \emph{finitary} and \emph{directed} automaton automorphisms, and showed that they form a generating set for the group of bounded automaton automorphisms $\BTd$, see \cref{prop:ba_gset}.

\begin{definition}
An automorphism $\phi \in \AutTd$ is \emph{finitary} if there exists some constant $N_\phi \in \N$ such that $\phi\at{v} = 1$ for each $v\in C\star$ with $|v|\geq N_\phi$.
The smallest constant for which this holds is the \emph{depth} of the automorphism $\phi$, denote as $\mathrm{depth}(\phi)$.
\end{definition}

Finitary automorphisms form a subgroup of $\BTd$ which we denote as $\FinTd$.
Examples of finitary automorphisms are given in \cref{fig:finitary-examples}

