\begin{definition}
An automorphism $\delta \in \AutTd \setminus \FinTd$ is \emph{directed} if there exists some $k \in \{1,2,...,d\}$ such that
\begin{equation}\label{eq:directed-automorphism-definition}
	\delta
	=
	(\phi_1, \phi_2, \ldots, \phi_{k-1}, \delta', \phi_{k+1}, \ldots, \phi_d) \cdot s
	\in
	\AutTd \wr \mathrm{Sym}(C)
\end{equation}
where each $\phi_j$ is finitary and $\delta'$ is a directed automorphism (that is, $\delta'$ is not finitary and can also be written in this form).
We say that the \emph{direction} of $\delta$ is the unique $c_k\in C$ such that $\delta\at{c_k}\notin \FinTd$, which we denote as $\mathrm{dir}(\delta)=c_k$.
The \emph{spine} of a directed automorphism, denoted $\mathrm{spine}(\delta)$, is defined as the unique infinite word such that $\mathrm{spine}(\delta) = \mathrm{dir}(\delta)\mathrm{spine}(\delta')$.
\end{definition}

We denote the set of all directed automaton automorphisms as $\DirTd$.
See \cref{fig:directed-examples} for some examples (in these example, $a$ and $b$ are as in \cref{fig:finitary-examples}).

