We then say that a derivation tree as in \cref{fig:derivation-tree} is a derivation tree labelled by $\alpha\beta\alpha\alpha\gamma$ and that for each word $w$ with $S \to^{\alpha\beta\alpha\alpha\gamma} w$, there is a derivation tree with the same labelling.
We then define a weaker version of unambiguous grammars as follows.

\begin{definition}
    Suppose we are given an ET0L grammar, then we say that the grammar is \emph{unambiguous with respect to its rational control} if for each word $w\in \Sigma\star$ with $S\to^\alpha w$ and $\alpha\in \mathcal{R}$, there is exactly one derivation tree for $w$ which is labelled by $\alpha$.
\end{definition}

Our aim in this section is to prove \cref{thm:gfun} and thus give a characterisation of the generating function for the languages derived in \cref{thm:main}.
In order to state this theorem, we must first define \emph{unambiguous limiting ET0L languages} as follows.

\begin{definition}\label{def:unambiguous-et0l}
    Let $E = (\Sigma, V, T,\mathcal{R}, S)$ be an ET0L grammar as in \cref{def:et0l grammar}, then we say that $E$ is \emph{unambiguous limiting} if
    \begin{enumerate}
        \item $T$ contains 3 tables, i.e., $T = \{\alpha,\beta,\gamma\}$;
        \item the rational control is given by $\mathcal{R} = \alpha\beta\star\gamma$;
        \item we have $\varepsilon \notin \beta(X)$ for each $X\in V$;
        \item\label{def:unambiguous-et0l/unambig}
        the language $L(E)$ is unambiguous with respect to its rational control, that is,
        for each word $w \in L(E)$ and each control $\rho_n = \alpha\beta^n\gamma$, there is at most one derivation tree for $w$ labelled by $\rho_n$; and
        \item\label{def:unambiguous-et0l/limit}
        if $S\to^{\rho_n} w$ for some $w\in L(E)$ where $\rho_n = \alpha\beta^n\gamma$, then there exists some $K \geq n$ such that $S\to^{\rho_k} w$ for each $k \geq K$.
    \end{enumerate}
    Condition \ref{def:unambiguous-et0l/unambig} ensures that the words are generated by the grammar in some unambiguous way, and condition \ref{def:unambiguous-et0l/limit} ensures that the language has some kind of limiting behaviour with respect to its tables.
\end{definition}

To simplify the proof of \cref{thm:main}, we require the following lemma.

\begin{lemma}\label{cor:well-defined}
Suppose that the group $G$ has a finite symmetric generating set $X$, and that $M\subseteq G$ is a subset of the group for which
$
    L = \{
        w\in X\star
    \mid
        \overline{w}\in M
    \}
$
is an ET0L language.
Then, for each subgroup $H \leq G$ with finite generating set $Y \subseteq H$, the set
$
    L'=\{
        w \in Y\star
    \mid
        \overline{w} \in M \cap H
    \}
$
forms an ET0L language.

Suppose that we are given an unambiguous limiting ET0L grammar for $L$ and an injective monoid homomorphism $\varphi\colon Y^* \to X^*$.
Then, it is computable to find an unambiguous limiting ET0L grammar for $L' = \varphi^{-1}(L)$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}
    The more general form of this proof follows from the fact that the family of ET0L languages is closed under taking inverse monoid homomorphsim~(see Corollary 3.2 in \cite{culik1974}).
    For the second half of this theorem statement, we repeat this proof as follows.

    Let $E = (X, V,T,\mathcal R, S)$ be an unambiguous limiting ET0L grammar for the language $L$.
    In particular, suppose $T = \{\alpha,\beta,\gamma\}$ with $\mathcal R = \alpha\beta^*\gamma$.
    Moreover, suppose we are given an injective monoid homomorphism $\varphi\colon Y^* \to X^*$ as in the lemma statement.
    In the remainder of this proof, we give a brief outline of the construction of an unambiguous limiting grammar $E' = (Y, V',T',\mathcal R', S')$ for the language $L' = \varphi^{-1}(L)$.

    We begin by labelling the letters of $Y$ as
    $
        Y
        =
        \{
            y_1, y_2,\ldots,y_m
        \},
    $
    and writing
    \[
        \varphi(y_i) = w_i = w_{i,1} w_{i,2} \cdots w_{i,k_i} \in X^*
    \]
    for the projection of each letter $y_i$ by the map $\varphi$, where each $w_{i,j}\in X$.
    To simplify notation later in this proof, we write $W = \{w_1,w_2,\ldots,w_m\}$.
    From this, we construct the set of nonterminals $V'$ as follows.

    \medskip

    \noindent\underline{\textit{Nonterminals}}.

    \smallskip
    \noindent
    For each nonterminal $A\in V$, each $x \in X$, and each pair of letters $y_a,y_b\in Y$, possibly with $a=b$, we introduce the nonterminals
    $
        A_{(a,c), (b,d)}, L_{x,(a,c)} \in V'
    $
    to the grammar $E'$
    for each $c\in \{1,2,\ldots, k_a\}$ and $d \in \{1,2,\ldots,k_b\}$.
    Moreover, for each nonterminal $A\in V$, we introduce an additional nonterminal $A_\varepsilon\in V'$.
    The nonterminals of the form $A_{(a,c),(b,d)}\in V'$ correspond to words of the form
    \begin{equation}\label{eq:word1}
    \begin{aligned}
        &w_{a,c} w_{a,c+1}\cdots w_{a,k_a}
        W^*
        w_{b,1} w_{b,2}\cdots w_{b,d}
        \quad\text{or additionally}
        \\&
        w_{a,c} w_{a,c+1} \cdots w_{a_d}\quad\text{if}\quad a=b\text{ and }c\leq d.
    \end{aligned}
    \end{equation}
    The nonterminals of the form $L_{x,(a,c)}$ correspond to an instance of the letter $x = w_{a,c}$.
    The nonterminals of the form $A_\varepsilon\in V'$ correspond to the empty word.
    Lastly, the set $V'$ will contain a starting symbol $S'\in V'$.
    It is clear that $E'$ has a finite number of nonterminals.

    \medskip

    \noindent\underline{\textit{Tables}}.

    \smallskip
    \noindent
    We define a table $\alpha'\colon (V'\cup Y)^*\to \Reg(V'\cup Y)$ for the grammar $E'$ such that $S' \cdot \alpha' \subseteq (V')^*$ is obtained by taking the words $u\in S\cdot \alpha$ and replacing its letters with labelled versions as in $V'$ such that the resulting word corresponds to a word in $W^*$.
    Notice that $S'\cdot \alpha'$ is a regular language as it can be obtained by applying a certain nondetermininstic finite-state transduction to the regular language $S\cdot \alpha$.
    All other mappings of the table $\alpha'$ are unimportant for the remainder of this proof.

    We define a table $\beta'\colon (V'\cup Y)^*\to\Reg(V'\cup Y)$ as follows.
    \begin{itemize}
    \item For each nonterminal $L_{x,a,c} \in V'$, we have $L_{x,(a,c)} \cdot \beta' = L_{x,(a,c)}$.
    \item For each $A_\varepsilon \in V'$, we obtain $A_\varepsilon \cdot \beta'$ by selecting all words $u \in (A\cdot \beta) \cap V^*$ and subscripting each nonterminal in $u$ with the empty word. 
    \item For each $A_{(a,c),(b,d)}$, we obtain $A_{(a,c),(b,d)}\cdot \beta'$ by taking all the words $u \in (A\cdot \beta)$ which can be annotated to represent a word as in (\ref{eq:word1}), then replacing each such letter with an appropriate letter from $V'$.
    \end{itemize}
    In the last two cases as above, we notice that the resulting sets of words are regular languages as they can be obtained by from a nondeterministic finite-state transduction of the regular language $A\cdot \beta$.
    We then repeat this construction to obtain a table $\gamma'\colon (V'\cup Y)^*\to\Reg(V'\cup Y)$ from $\gamma$.

    Notice then that each word $u \in S'\cdot (\alpha'(\beta')^*\gamma')$ belongs to the free monoid $(V')^*$.
    Moreover, if $u$ only contains nonterminals of the form $L_{x,(a,c)}$, then the word $u$ corresponds to a word $v\in S\cdot (\alpha\beta^*\gamma) \cap W^*$, that is, a word which belongs to the image $\varphi(Y^*)$ where each letter is annotated with the letter of which it is an image.
    Thus, we introduce one final table $\delta$ as such that for each $B\in V'$, we have
    \[
        B\cdot \delta=
        \begin{cases}
            y_a &\text{if }B = L_{x,(a,1)}\text{ for some }a\\
            \varepsilon &\text{if }B=L_{x,(a,c)}\text{ for some }a\text{ and }c\neq 1\\
            B&\text{otherwise}.
        \end{cases}
    \]
    Notice then that
    \[
        L' = S'\cdot(\alpha' (\beta')^* \gamma'\delta).
    \]
    Moreover, notice that $\gamma'' = \gamma'\delta$ can be viewed as a table of the grammar $E'$.

    \medskip

    \noindent\underline{\textit{Grammar construction}}.

    \smallskip
    \noindent
    Thus, $L'$ can be generated by an unambiguous limiting ET0L grammar $E' = (Y, V', T', \mathcal R', S')$ with $T = \{\alpha',\beta', \gamma''\}$ and $\mathcal R' = \alpha' (\beta')^*\gamma''$.
    Moreover, it is clear from our construction, and the fact that $\varphi$ is injective, that $E'$ is an unambiguous limiting ET0L grammar.
    Furthermore, each step of this proof is constructive and computable
\end{proof}

From the above lemma, we have the following technical corollary which is used in the proof of our main theorem.

\begin{corollary}\label{cor:well-defined2}
Suppose that the group $G$ has a finite symmetric generating set $X$, and that $M\subseteq G$ is a subset of the group for which
$
    L = \{
        w\in X\star
    \mid
        \overline{w}\in M
    \}
$
is an unambiguous limiting ET0L language.
Then, for each subgroup $H \leq G$ with finite generating set $Y \subseteq H$, the set
$
    L'=\{
        w \in Y\star
    \mid
        \overline{w} \in M \cap H
    \}
$
forms an ET0L language.
Moreover, suppose that the word problem for $G$ is solvable.

Then, there exists an algorithm which, given as input a group embedding $\psi\colon H\hookrightarrow G$ specified by giving $\psi(y)$ for each $y\in Y$, and a description of an unambiguous limiting ET0L grammar for $L$, can generate as output an unambiguous limiting ET0L language for $L'$.
\end{corollary}

\begin{proof}
We begin by modifying the group embedding $\psi$ to produce an injective monoid homomorphism $\varphi\colon Y^* \to X^*$ as follows.

For each generator $y\in Y$ select a distinct nontrivial geodesic word $w_y\in X^*$.
We then write $(w_y)^{-1}$ for the inverse of $w_y$ (i.e.\@ the inverse as taken in the free group $F_X$).
Notice that a choice of such words $w_y$ is computable since the word problem for $G$ is solvable.

We then define the map $\varphi\colon Y^* \to X^*$ such that
\[
    \varphi(y) = w_y (w_y)^{-1} \psi(y)\in X^*
\]
for each $y\in Y$.
It can then be shown that the map $\varphi$ is an injective monoid homomorphism which presents the same group embedding as $\psi$.

Our corollary then follows from \cref{cor:well-defined}.
\end{proof}


\subsection{Generating Functions}\label{sec:gfun}

In order to prove Theorem~\ref{thm:gfun} we require the following additional lemma on the multivariate generating function of regular languages.
The proof of this lemma is well-known, for a self-contained proof of this, see Lemma~2.5 in \cite{bishop2023groups}.

\begin{lemma}[Lemma~2.5 in \cite{bishop2023groups}]\label{lem:rational-gfun}
    The multivariate generating function of a regular language is rational.
\end{lemma}

We are now ready to prove our theorem on the generating functions of ET0L languages as follows.

\TheoremGFun

\begin{proof}
Let $E = (\Sigma,V,T,\mathcal{R},S)$ be an ET0L language as in \cref{def:unambiguous-et0l} where $V = \{X_1=S,X_2,...,X_k\}$, $T = \{\alpha,\beta,\gamma\}$ and $\mathcal{R} = \alpha\beta\star\gamma$.
Moreover, suppose
\[
    \alpha\colon
    \left\{
    \begin{aligned}
        X_1 &\mapsto L_{\alpha,1}\\
        X_2 &\mapsto L_{\alpha,2}\\
        &\rvdots\\
        X_k &\mapsto L_{\alpha,k}
    \end{aligned}
    \right.
    ,\qquad
    \beta\colon
    \left\{
    \begin{aligned}
        X_1 &\mapsto L_{\beta,1}\\
        X_2 &\mapsto L_{\beta,2}\\
        &\rvdots\\
        X_k &\mapsto L_{\beta,k}
    \end{aligned}
    \right.
    \qquad\text{and}\qquad
    \gamma\colon
    \left\{
    \begin{aligned}
        X_1 &\mapsto L_{\gamma,1}\\
        X_2 &\mapsto L_{\gamma,2}\\
        &\rvdots\\
        X_k &\mapsto L_{\gamma,k}
    \end{aligned}
    \right.
\]
where each $L_{\alpha,i}$, $L_{\beta,i}$ and $L_{\gamma,i}$ is a regular language over $V\cup \Sigma$.

In the remainder of this proof, we write $\mathbf{x}$ for the tuple of variables $(x_1,x_2,...,x_k)$ where each variable $x_i$ corresponds to the variable $X_i \in V$.
Suppose that $m=|\Sigma|$, we then write $\mathbf{y}$ for the tuple of variables $(y_1,y_2,...,y_m)$ where each variable $y_i$ corresponds to a letter $\sigma_i$ in $\Sigma = \{\sigma_1, \sigma_2, ...,\sigma_m\}$.

For the regular languages $L_{\alpha,i}$, $L_{\beta,i}$ and $L_{\gamma,i}$ we write $h_{\alpha,i}(\mathbf{x},\mathbf{y})$, $h_{\beta,i}(\mathbf{x},\mathbf{y})$ $h_{\gamma,i}(\mathbf{x},\mathbf{y})$ for their generating functions, respectively.
From \cref{lem:rational-gfun}, we know that these generating functions are rational.

Then, for each $h_{\gamma,i}$, we define a function $H_{\gamma,i}(z)$ as
\[
    H_{\gamma,i}(z)
    =
    h_{\gamma,i}(
            \underbrace{0,0,...,0}_{k\text{ times}},
            \underbrace{z,z,...,z}_{m\text{ times}}
        ).
\]
We then see that the generating function can be written as
\[
    f(z) = g(
        H_{\gamma,1}(z),
        H_{\gamma,2}(z),...,
        H_{\gamma,k}(z),
        z,z,...,z
    )
\]
with
\[
    g(\mathbf{x},\mathbf{y})
    =
    \lim_{m\to\infty} g_m(\mathbf{x},\mathbf{y})
\]
where
\begin{align*}
    g_0(\mathbf{x},\mathbf{y})&= h_{\alpha,1}(\mathbf{x},\mathbf{y})
    \quad
    \text{ and }
    \\g_{m+1}(\mathbf{x},\mathbf{y}) &= g_{m}(
        h_{\beta,1}(\mathbf{x},\mathbf{y}),
        h_{\beta,2}(\mathbf{x},\mathbf{y}),
        ...,
        h_{\beta,k}(\mathbf{x},\mathbf{y}),
        \mathbf{y}
    )
\end{align*}
for each $m \geq 0$.
\end{proof}

\section{Main theorem}\label{sec:main}

The aim of this section is to prove \cref{thm:main}.

In this section, let $G\leq \AutTd$ be a bounded automata group with a finite generating set $\Sigma = F\cup D$ where $F \subset \FinTd$ and $D \subset \DirTd$.
Moreover, let $\eta\in C^\omega$ be an eventually periodic ray in the tree $\Td$.

\subsection{Quasi-periodic diverging rays}

Recall that the vertices of the tree $\mathcal T_d$ are labelled with words from the set $C^*$ where $C = \{c_1,c_2,...,c_d\}$.
Moreover, the rays are of the form $C^\omega$.
In the proof of our main theorem, we will only need to consider a finite set of eventually periodic rays as specified by a finite set $I\subset C^* \times C^*$ as described in the following definition.
Later in \cref{lem:E_wlog1,lem:E_wlog2,lem:E_wlog3}, we refine this set to ensure that it has a number of desirable properties.

\begin{definition}\label{def:setE}
We define a finite set $I\subset C^*\times C^*$ such that, for each
\[
    \zeta \in \{\eta\} \cup \mathrm{spine}(D)\cup (\mathrm{spine}(D)\cdot D),
\]
where $\mathrm{spine}(D) = \{\mathrm{spine}(\delta)\mid \delta\in D\}$,
there exists some $(u,v)\in I$ such that $\zeta = u v^\omega$.
Moreover, we require that $I$ is minimal in the sense that we cannot remove any element and still have all of the above statements hold.
In particular, this implies that, $|v|\geq 1$ for each $(u,v) \in I$.
\end{definition}

In this section, we write $\zeta = (u,v)\in I$ to denote the eventually periodic ray $\zeta = uv^\omega$.
We believe this notation simplifies the proof of \cref{thm:main}.

Notice that there may be many choices for such a set $I$ as in \cref{def:setE}.
In the following lemmas we refine our choice of such a set so that it has some desired properties which we find essential to the proof of our main theorem.

\begin{lemma}\label{lem:E_wlog1}
We may assume without loss of generality that there exists some constants $\ell \geq 1$ such that for each $(u,v) \in I$, we have $|u| = |v| = \ell$, and that $I$ satisfies the requirements given in \cref{def:setE}.

Moreover, given such a set $I$ as in \cref{def:setE} it is effectively computable to construct another set $I'$ which satisfies the additional properties as above.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}
Let $m\geq 0$ be the constant defined as
\[
    m
    =
    \max\{
        |u|
    \mid
        (u,v) \in I
    \}.
\]
Now let $\ell\geq 1$ be defined as
\[
    \ell = \mathrm{lcm}(
        \{|v| \mid (u,v)\in I\}
        \cup
        \{m\}
    ).
\]
We now construct a finite set $I'$ as follows.
For each $(u,v)\in I$, we define $u'$ as the length-$\ell$ prefix of $uv^\omega$, and we define $v'$ to be a cyclic permutation of the word $v$ for which $u'(v')^\omega = uv^\omega$.
We add $(u',(v')^{\ell/|v'|})$ to the set $I'$.

We now see that if $(a,b)\in I'$, then $|a|=|b|=\ell$ and that $I'$ represents the same elements as $I$.
Moreover, we see that $I'$ is minimal since for each  $(a,b),(a',b')\in I'$, we have $ab^\omega = a'(b')^\omega$ if and only if $(a,b)=(a',b')$.

Notice that the above steps are computable.
Thus, given a set $I$, there is an algorithm which can construct the set $I'$.
We thus replace $I$ with $I'$ and conclude our proof.
\end{proof}

\begin{lemma}\label{lem:E_wlog2}
In addition to the properties given in \cref{lem:E_wlog1}, we may assume without loss of generality that each prefix $u$ in $(u,v)\in I$ is distinct.
That is, for each $(u,v),(u',v')\in I$ with $(u,v)\neq (u',v')$, we have $u\neq u'$.

Moreover, given a set $I$ as in \cref{lem:E_wlog1}, it is effectively computable to construct another set $I'$ which also satisfies the above requirements.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}
From \cref{lem:E_wlog1}, we may assume that there is some $\ell\geq 1$ such that for each $(u,v) \in I$, we have $|u|=|v|=\ell$.
Thus, for each $(u,v),(u',v')\in I$ with $(u,v)\neq (u',v')$, we have $uv\neq u'v'$.
We construct a set $I'\subset C^*\times C^*$ as
\[
    I'
    =
    \{
        (uv,vv)
    \mid
        (u,v)\in I
    \}.
\]
We then see that the set $I'$ has our desired properties.

Notice that the above steps are computable, that is, given a set $I$, there is an algorithm which can construct the set $I'$.
Thus, we replace $I$ with $I'$, and $\ell$ with $2\ell$ and conclude our proof.
\end{proof}

\begin{lemma}\label{lem:E_wlog3}
Suppose that $I$ is a set as in \cref{def:setE} which satisfies the properties as in \cref{lem:E_wlog1,lem:E_wlog2} for some constant $\ell\geq 1$.
Then, we may also assume without loss of generality that $I$ satisfies the following properties.
For each $x\in D\subseteq \Sigma$, there is a choice of words $u_x,v_x\in C^*$ such that $\mathrm{spine}(x)=u_x v_x^\omega$ with $x\at u_x = x\at u_x v_x$ and 
\[
    \ell\geq |u_x|
    \qquad
    \text{ and }
    \qquad
    \ell \equiv 0 \pmod{|v_x|}.
\]
Moreover, for each finitary automorphism $f\in F \subseteq \Sigma$, we have $\ell\geq \mathrm{depth}(f)$. 

Given a set $I$ as in \cref{lem:E_wlog2}, it is effectively computable to construct another set $I'$ which also satisfies the above requirements.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}
For each $x\in D$, choose words $u_x,v_x \in C^*$ such that $\mathrm{spine}(x)=u_x v_x^\omega$ with $x\at u_x = x\at u_x v_x$.
From this, we then define a constant $\ell'\geq 1$ as
\[
    \ell'
    =
    \mathrm{lcm}
    \left(
            \{ \ell \}
            \cup
            \{ |u_x| \mid x\in D \}
            \cup
            \{ |v_x| \mid x\in D \}
            \cup
            \{ \mathrm{depth}(f) \mid f\in F \}
    \right).
\]
We now define a set $I'$ as
\[
    I'
    =
    \{
        (uv^{(\ell'/\ell) - 1}, v^{\ell'/\ell})
    \mid
        (u,v)\in I
    \}.
\]
We then see that this new set $I'$ satisfies the properties described in \cref{lem:E_wlog1,lem:E_wlog2} and satisfies our new constraint.

Notice that the above steps are computable, that is, given a set $I$ and a description of the generators $D$, there is an algorithm which can construct the set $I'$.
Thus, after replacing $I$ with $I'$ and $\ell$ with $\ell'$ we conclude our proof.
\end{proof}

\begin{definition}\label{def:quasiperiodic_diverging}
Let $I$ be a finite set as in \cref{def:setE} which satisfies the properties as described in \cref{lem:E_wlog1,lem:E_wlog2,lem:E_wlog3} for some constant $\ell\geq 1$.
We then say that $I$ is a set of \emph{$\ell$-quasi-periodic diverging rays}.
\end{definition}

\subsection{Portrait depth}

Suppose that $x\in \AutTd$, then the \emph{portrait} of $x$ is the minimal subtree $P_x$ of $\Td$ such that the root is in $P_x$, if $v \in C^*$ is a leaf of $P_x$, then $x\at v = 1$.
We have already seen examples of portraits as given in \cref{fig:directed-examples,fig:finitary-examples}.
Notice here that when we say \textit{minimal subtree}, we mean that there is no proper subtree of $P_x$ with the same properties.
Moreover, we note here that it is possible for a portrait to be infinite, as in \cref{fig:directed-examples}.
We notice then that each automorphism $x\in \AutTd$ has a unique minimal portrait $P_x$.

\begin{figure}[ht!]
\centering
\includegraphics{figure/grigorchukb}
\caption{Portrait of the generator $b$ of Grigorchuk group (left), and an overlay of the ray $\zeta = 11101...$ on top of this portrait (right).}\label{fig:grig-gen-b}
\end{figure}

Then, given an infinite ray $\zeta \in C^\omega$, we define its \emph{portrait depth} with respect to some automorphism $x$ by overlaying $\zeta$ on top the portrait $P_x$ and calculating the depth at which the ray reaches a leaf of the portrait.
For example, overlaying the ray $\zeta = 11101...$ on the portrait given in the left-hand side of \cref{fig:grig-gen-b}, we obtain the diagram as given in the right-hand side of \cref{fig:grig-gen-b}.
We then define the portrait depth in this case to be 5.
More formally, we define the portrait depth equivalently as follows.

\begin{definition}\label{def:compdepth}
The \emph{portrait depth} of rays with respect to automorphism is given by the function
$\CompDepth\colon C^\omega \times \AutTd \to \mathbb N \cup \{\infty\}$ define as
\[
    \CompDepth(\zeta,x)
    =
    \min\{
        |v|
    \mid
        v\in C^* \text{ is a prefix of }\zeta\text{ with }x\at v =1
    \}
\]
with $\CompDepth(\zeta,x) = \infty$ if $x\at{v}\neq 1$ for each prefix $v$ of $\zeta$.
\end{definition}

Notice here that the portrait depth can be infinite, for example, if $b$ is the generator as in the Grigorchuk group, then we find that $\CompDepth(1^\omega,b) = \infty$.

Suppose that we have a word $w = w_1 w_2\cdots w_k\in \Sigma^*$ and a ray $\zeta \in C^\omega$, and we want to compute the action $\zeta \acts w$.
Then, there exists a sequence of finite rays $\zeta_1,\zeta_2,...,\zeta_{k+1}$ 
such that
    $\zeta_1 = \zeta$,
    $\zeta_{i+1} = \zeta_{i}\acts w_{i}$ for each $i\in \{1,2,...,k\}$
    and thus
    $\zeta_{k+1} = \zeta\acts w$.
Thus, we may uniquely decorate our word $w$ as
\[
    \Decorate(\zeta,w)\coloneqq
    w_1^{(a_1)}
    w_2^{(a_2)}
    \cdots
    w_k^{(a_k)}
\quad\text{where each}\quad
    a_i = \CompDepth(\zeta_i, w_i).
\]
Note that each of these symbols $w_i^{(a_i)}$ belongs to a disjoint copy $\Sigma^{(a_i)}$ of the generating set $\Sigma$.
These annotated words are useful for simplifying the explanations of our proof of \cref{thm:main}.
We note here that the sequence $a_1,a_2,...,a_k$ is not necessarily increasing or decreasing, in fact, this sequence can go from finite, to infinite, back to finite.

\begin{lemma}\label{lem:decoration-prefix}
Let $w = w_1 w_2 \cdots w_k \in \Sigma^*$ and let $\zeta \in C^\omega$.
Suppose that
\[
    \Decorate(\zeta,w)
    =
    w_1^{(a_1)}
    w_2^{(a_2)}
    \cdots
    w_k^{(a_k)}
\]
where each $a_i$ is finite, and that $A = \max\{a_1,a_2,...,a_k\}$.
Then, after factoring $\zeta$ as $\zeta = p \zeta'$ with $|p| = A$ and $\zeta'\in C^\omega$, we find that
\[
    \Decorate(p\alpha, w) = \Decorate(\zeta,w)
\]
for each $\alpha\in C^\omega$.
That is, if the word is decorated by finite values, then we only need to know a finite-length prefix of $\zeta$ to compute the decorations.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}
From the definition of the decorated word and the portrait depth, we see that there exists some sequence of rays $\zeta_1,\zeta_2,...,\zeta_k, \zeta_{k+1}\in C^\omega$ for which $\zeta=\zeta_1$, and $\zeta_{i+1} = \zeta_i \acts w_i$ for each $i$.
Moreover, these rays have the property that $\CompDepth(\zeta_i,w_i) = a_i\leq A$ for each $i$.

Let $p_1,p_2,...,p_k,p_{k+1}$ be the length $A$ prefixes of the rays $\zeta_1,\zeta_2,...,\zeta_{k+1}$, and let $\alpha\in C^\omega$
be an arbitrary ray.
From the definition of $\CompDepth$, we then see that $(p_i\alpha)\acts w_i = p_{i+1}\alpha$ and $\CompDepth(p_i\alpha, w_i) = a_i$ for each $i$.
Let $p=p_1$ then from the definition of decorated words, we see that
\[
    \Decorate(p\alpha, w)
    =
    w_1^{(a_1)}
    w_2^{(a_2)}
    \cdots
    w_k^{(a_k)}
    = \Decorate(\zeta,w)
\]
as desired.
\end{proof}

We extend our notation for the portrait depth to prefixes of infinite rays by defining the function
$
    \CompDepthfin\colon C^*\times \AutTd \to \mathbb N \cup \{\infty\}
$
as
\[
    \CompDepthfin(u, x)
    \coloneqq
    \begin{cases}
        \CompDepth(uc^\omega , x) & \text{if }\CompDepth(uc^\omega , x) \leq |u|
        \\
        \infty &\text{otherwise}
    \end{cases}
\]
where $c\in C$ is any fixed element. From \cref{lem:decoration-prefix}, we know that the above definition does not change depending on the choice of letter $c$.

Moreover, we also define a function $\Decoratefin$ as follows.
Suppose that $u \in C^*$ is a path in the tree, $w = w_1 w_2 \cdots w_k \in \Sigma^*$ is a word, and $c \in C$ is some fixed direction in the tree.
With
\[
    \Decorate(uc^\omega, w)
    =
    w_1^{(a_1)} w_2^{(a_2)} \cdots w_k^{(a_k)},
\]
we then define
\[
    \Decoratefin(u, w)
    \coloneqq
    w_1^{(b_1)} w_2^{(b_2)} \cdots w_k^{(b_k)}
    \quad
    \text{where each}
    \quad
    b_i = \begin{cases}
        a_i &\text{if }a_i \leq |u|\\
        \infty &\text{otherwise}
    \end{cases}.
\]
We then notice that from the definition of $\Decorate$ and \cref{lem:decoration-prefix}, that this definition does not depend on the choice of letter $c$ as above.

\subsection{Proof of main theorem}

We are now ready to prove our main theorem as follows.

\TheoremMain

\begin{proof}
From \cref{cor:well-defined2,lem:fg.ss.ba_overgroup} and the fact that the word problem is solvable for bounded automata groups (see~Corollary 1 in~\cite{Bondarenko2011}), it is sufficient to consider the case where $G$ is a bounded automata group with a finite 
generating set which can be partitioned as $\Sigma = F\cup D$ where $F \subset \FinTd$ is a set of finitary automorphisms and $D \subset \DirTd$ is a set of directed automorphisms.

We note that in this proof, our group acts on infinite rays on the right.
We choose this convention to simplify our proofs.

Suppose $\eta \in C^\omega$ is an eventually periodic ray.
Let $I\subseteq C^*\times C^*$ be a set of $\ell$-quasi-periodic rays as defined in \cref{def:quasiperiodic_diverging}.
We will make use of this set when defining the set of non-terminals for our languages.
It is important here to remark that the set $I$ is finite, as this will then imply that we have finitely many non-terminals as required by the definition of ET0L grammars.

We note here that the set $I\subseteq C^*\times C^*$ is effectively computable as a result of \cref{lem:E_wlog1,lem:E_wlog2,lem:E_wlog3}.

In this proof, we construct two ET0L grammars $E = (\Sigma,V,T,\mathcal R, S)$ and $E' = (\Sigma,V,T,\mathcal R, S')$ for the languages $\WP(G,X,\Stab(\eta))$ and $\Sigma^*\setminus \WP(G,X,\Stab(\eta))$, respectively.
Notice that the only difference between these two grammars is their start symbols $S$ and $S'$, respectively.

Our grammar has three tables $T = \{\tau_\mathrm{init}, \tau_\mathrm{up}, \tau_\mathrm{finish}\}$ and rational control of the form $\mathcal{R} = \tau_\mathrm{init} (\tau_\mathrm{up})^* \tau_\mathrm{finish}$.
Moreover, in this proof we will show that the grammars which we construct satisfy the restrictions given in \cref{def:unambiguous-et0l}.

We begin our construction by introducing our nonterminals as follows.

\medskip
\noindent\textit{\underline{1.~Nonterminals and starting symbols}}.

\smallskip\noindent
We begin by introducing the starting symbols $S = \llbracket \eta ; \eta \rrbracket$ and $S' = \llbracket \eta ; \neg\eta \rrbracket$ of the grammars $E$ and $E'$, respectively.
The meaning of this notation will become clear after introducing the remaining non-terminals.

We now define the remaining nonterminals as follows.

\medskip
\noindent\textit{1.1.~part 1}.

\smallskip\noindent
For each pair of rays $\zeta=uv^\omega,\zeta'=u'(v')^\omega$ where $(u,v),(u'v')\in I$ and each pair of paths $p,p'\in C^{2\ell}$, we have nonterminals $\llbracket \zeta, p ; \zeta',p'\rrbracket$ and $\llbracket \zeta,p; \neg \eta \rrbracket$.
In our proof, we construct our grammars such that for each $m \geq 0$, we have
\[
    w\in \Sigma^*\cap
    (
    \llbracket \zeta, p ; \zeta',p'\rrbracket
    \cdot
    (\tau_\mathrm{up})^m \tau_\mathrm{finish}
    )
\]
if and only if $u v^m p\acts \overline{w} = u'(v')^m p'$ and
\[
    \Decoratefin(uv^mp, w)
    =
    w_1^{(a_1)} w_2^{(a_2)}\cdots w_k^{(a_k)}
\]
where each $a_i \leq |uv^mp| = (m+3)\ell$.
Moreover, our grammar ensures that, for each $m \geq 0$, we have
\[
    w\in \Sigma^*\cap (
    \llbracket \zeta, p ; \zeta',p'\rrbracket
    \cdot
    (\tau_\mathrm{up})^m \tau_\mathrm{finish})
\]
if and only if $u v^m p\acts \overline{w} \neq ab^{m+2}$ with $(a,b)\in I$ and $ab^\omega = \eta$, and
\[
    \Decoratefin(uv^mp, w)
    =
    w_1^{(a_1)} w_2^{(a_2)}\cdots w_k^{(a_k)}
\]
where each $a_i \leq |uv^mp| = (m+3)\ell$.

Notice that with $ab^\omega=\eta$ where $(a,b)\in I$, we see that the nonterminals $\llbracket\eta,b^2;\eta,b^2\rrbracket$ and $\llbracket \eta,b^2; \neg\eta\rrbracket$ can be used to generate the subset of word in $\WP(G,X,\Stab(\eta))$ and $\Sigma^*\setminus \WP(G,X,\Stab(\eta))$, respectively, where each letter has finite portrait depth.

\medskip
\noindent\textit{1.2.~part 2}.

\smallskip\noindent
For each ray $\zeta=uv^\omega$ with $(u,v)\in I$ and each path $p\in C^{2\ell}$, we introduce a non-terminal $\llbracket \zeta,p; \mathrm{any}\rrbracket$.
We construct our grammar such that for each $m\geq 0$, we have
\[
    w \in \Sigma^* \cap ( \llbracket \zeta,p;\mathrm{any}\rrbracket\cdot (\tau_\mathrm{up})^m \tau_\mathrm{finish})
\]
if and only if
\[
    \Decoratefin(uv^mp, w)
    =
    w_1^{(a_1)} w_2^{(a_2)}\cdots w_k^{(a_k)}
\]
where each $a_i \leq |uv^mp| = (m+3)\ell$.
Notice here that we place no restriction on where the action of the word $w$ takes the vertex $uv^mp$.

We require these additional nonterminals for reasons which will become clear when we construct the table $\tau_\mathrm{up}$.

\medskip
\noindent\textit{1.3.~part 3}. \textit{An explanation of the starting symbols.}

\smallskip\noindent
We construct the tables of our grammar such that for each $m\geq 0$, we have
\[
    w\in \Sigma^*\cap ( \llbracket \eta; \eta\rrbracket\cdot\tau_\mathrm{init}(\tau_\mathrm{up})^m \tau_\mathrm{finish})
\]
if and only if $w\in \mathcal \WP(G,X,\Stab(\eta))$ and
\[
    \Decorate(\eta, w)
    =
    w_1^{(a_1)} w_2^{(a_2)}\cdots w_k^{(a_k)}
\]
where each $a_i \in \{0,1,2,3,...,(m+3)\ell\}\cup \{\infty\}$.
Moreover, for each $m\geq 0$,
\[
    w\in \Sigma^*\cap ( \llbracket \eta; \neg\eta\rrbracket\cdot\tau_\mathrm{init}(\tau_\mathrm{up})^m \tau_\mathrm{finish})
\]
if and only if $w\in \Sigma^*\setminus \WP(G,X,\Stab(\eta))$ and
\[
    \Decorate(\eta, w)
    =
    w_1^{(a_1)} w_2^{(a_2)}\cdots w_k^{(a_k)}
\]
where each $a_i \in \{0,1,2,3,...,(m+3)\ell\}\cup \{\infty\}$.

\medskip
\noindent\textit{1.4.~Computability.}

\smallskip\noindent
Notice that given the set $I\subset C^*\times C^*$ and a description of the ray $\eta = ab^\omega$, we can list out all of the potential nonterminals, that is, the list of nonterminals is effectively computable.

\medskip
\noindent\textit{\underline{2.~Initial table $\tau_\mathrm{init}$}}.

\smallskip\noindent
Given any word $w = w_1 w_2 \cdots w_k \in \Sigma^*$, we may decorate its letters as
\[
    \Decorate(\eta,w)
    =
    w_1^{(a_1)} w_2^{(a_2)} \cdots w_k^{(a_k)}
\]
where each $a_i \in \mathbb{N}\cup \{\infty\}$.
We then factor the word $w$ uniquely as
\begin{equation}\label{eq:initial-table}
    w = z_0 x_1 z_1 x_2 z_2 \cdots x_q z_q
\end{equation}
where each $z_i \in \Sigma^*$ contains the sections of $w$ where each letter has finite portrait depth, and each $x_i \in \Sigma$ is a letter of $w$ which has infinite portrait depth.
Notice then that from the definition of portrait depth in \cref{def:compdepth}, we see that each $x_i$ must be a directed automorphism in $D = \Sigma\cap \DirTd$.

\medskip
\noindent\textit{2.1.~Map for initialising $E$}.

\smallskip\noindent
Let $\eta = ab^\omega$ where $(a,b)\in I$, then from the factorisation in (\ref{eq:initial-table}) we define the regular language $\llbracket \eta, \eta\rrbracket\cdot\tau_\mathrm{init}$ to contain all words of the form
\begin{multline*}
        \llbracket \eta, b^2 ; \alpha_1, (v_1)^2 \rrbracket 
        x_1
        \llbracket \alpha_1', (v_1')^2 ; \alpha_2, (v_2)^2 \rrbracket
        x_2
        \llbracket \alpha_2', (v_2')^2 ; \alpha_3, (v_3)^2 \rrbracket
        \\
        x_3
        \llbracket \alpha_3', (v_3')^2 ; \alpha_4, (v_4)^2 \rrbracket
        \cdots
        x_k
        \llbracket \alpha_k', (v_k')^2 ; \eta, b^2 \rrbracket
    \in
    (\llbracket \eta, \eta\rrbracket\cdot \tau_\mathrm{init})
\end{multline*}
where
\begin{enumerate}
\item\label{req:word-factor/item1}
$\mathrm{spine}(x_i) = \alpha_i = u_i (v_i)^\omega$ where $(u_i,v_i)\in I$
for each $i \in \{ 1,2,...,k \}$;
%
\item\label{req:word-factor/item2}
$\mathrm{spine}(x_i)\cdot x_i = \alpha_i' = u_i' (v_i')^\omega$ 
where $(u_i',v_i')\in I$
for each $i \in \{ 1,2,...,k \}$;
%
\item\label{req:word-factor/item3}
$b = v_1 = v_k'$ and $v'_i = v_{i+1}$ for each $i\in \{1,2,...,k-1\}$.
\end{enumerate}
Notice here that each nonterminal of the form $\llbracket -,-;-,-\rrbracket$ in the above corresponds to some word $z_i$ as in the factorisation given in (\ref{eq:initial-table}).
Items~\ref{req:word-factor/item1} and~\ref{req:word-factor/item2} as above are required to ensure that each $x_i$ has infinite portrait depth; and item~\ref{req:word-factor/item3} as above ensures that the action of the word stabilises the ray $\eta$.

\medskip
\noindent\textit{2.1.~Map for initialising $E'$}.

\smallskip\noindent
Again, let $\eta = ab^\omega$ where $(a,b)\in I$, then from the factorisation in (\ref{eq:initial-table}) we define the regular language $\llbracket \eta,\neg\eta\rrbracket\cdot\tau_\mathrm{init}$ to contain all words
\begin{multline*}
        \llbracket \eta, b^2 ; \alpha_1, (v_1)^2 \rrbracket 
        x_1
        \llbracket \alpha_1', (v_1')^2 ; \alpha_2, (v_2)^2 \rrbracket
        x_2
        \llbracket \alpha_2', (v_2')^2 ; \alpha_3, (v_3)^2 \rrbracket
        \\
        x_3
        \llbracket \alpha_3', (v_3')^2 ; \alpha_4, (v_4)^2 \rrbracket
        \cdots
        x_k
        \llbracket \alpha_k', (v_k')^2 ; \underline{\varphi} \rrbracket
    \in
    (\llbracket \eta, \neg\eta\rrbracket\cdot\tau_\mathrm{init})
\end{multline*}
where
\begin{enumerate}
\item\label{req:word-factor2/item1}
$\mathrm{spine}(x_i) = \alpha_i = u_i(v_i)^\omega$ where $(u_i,v_i)\in I$
for each $i \in \{ 1,2,...,k \}$;
%
\item\label{req:word-factor2/item2}
$\mathrm{spine}(x_i)\cdot x_i = \alpha_i' = u_i' (v_i')^\omega$ where $(u_i',v_i')\in I$
for each $i \in \{ 1,2,...,k \}$;
%
\item\label{req:word-factor2/item3}
$b = v_2$ and $v'_i = v_{i+1}$ for each $i\in \{1,2,...,k-1\}$; and

\item\label{req:word-factor2/item4}
if $v'_k = b$, then $\underline{\varphi} = \neg \eta$, otherwise, $v'_k\neq b$ and $\underline{\varphi} = \mathrm{any}$.
\end{enumerate}
Notice here that each nonterminal in the above corresponds to some factor $z_i$ as in (\ref{eq:initial-table}).
Items~\ref{req:word-factor2/item1} and~\ref{req:word-factor2/item2} ensure that each $x_i$ has infinite portrait depth; item~\ref{req:word-factor2/item3} and~\ref{req:word-factor2/item4} ensure that the word has an action which does not stabilise the ray $\eta$.

\medskip
\noindent\textit{2.3.~$\tau_\mathrm{init}$ is a valid table}.

\smallskip\noindent
From our definition of $\tau_\mathrm{init}$ as above, we see that both $\llbracket \eta,\eta\rrbracket\cdot\tau_\mathrm{init}$ and $\llbracket \eta,\neg\eta\rrbracket\cdot\tau_\mathrm{init}$ are regular languages, and thus $\tau_\mathrm{init}$ is a table as in \cref{def:tables}.
We note here that for all other nonterminals, the table $\tau_\mathrm{init}$ does not need to be specified as this table will only be applied to the starting symbols.

\medskip
\noindent\textit{2.4.~Computability.}

\smallskip\noindent
Notice that given the set $I\subset C^*\times C^*$ and the set of nonterminals, there is an algorithm which can generate the finite state automata for the table $\tau_\mathrm{init}$.

\medskip
\noindent\textit{\underline{3.~Processing table $\tau_\mathrm{up}$}}.

\smallskip\noindent
We now describe the map $\tau_\mathrm{up}$ which performs replacements on the nonterminals of form $\llbracket \zeta,p; \zeta',p'\rrbracket$, $\llbracket \zeta,p ; \neg\eta\rrbracket$ and $\llbracket \zeta,p ; \mathrm{any} \rrbracket$.
Let $\zeta = uv^\omega$ where $(u,v)\in I$.
Recall from earlier in our proof, when we introduce the nonterminals, that it is our intention with this construction that if $w$ belongs to one of
\begin{multline*}
    \Sigma^*\cap (\llbracket \zeta,p; \zeta',p'\rrbracket\cdot(\tau_\mathrm{up})^m \tau_\mathrm{finish}),\\
    \Sigma^*\cap (\llbracket \zeta,p ; \neg\eta\rrbracket\cdot(\tau_\mathrm{up})^m \tau_\mathrm{finish})
    \quad\text{or}\\
    \Sigma^*\cap (\llbracket \zeta,p ; \mathrm{any} \rrbracket\cdot(\tau_\mathrm{up})^m \tau_\mathrm{finish}),
\end{multline*}
for some $m\geq 0$, then we may decorate the word $w$ as
\[
    \Decoratefin(uv^mp,w) = w_1^{(a_1)} w_2^{(a_2)} \cdots w_k^{(a_k)}
\]
where each $a_i \leq |uv^mp| = (m+3)\ell$.
Notice then that we may factor such a word $w$ as
\begin{equation}\label{eq:processing-factorisation}
    w = z_0 x_1 z_1 x_2 z_2 \cdots x_s z_s
\end{equation}
where each $z_i\in \Sigma^*$ contains the letters $w_i$ of the word $w$ for which $a_i \leq (m+2)\ell$, and each $x_i$ is a letter $w_i$ of the word for which the portrait depth is bound as $(m+2)\ell < a_i \leq (m+3)\ell$.
We see that such a factorisation is well-defined and unique.
Moreover, from \cref{lem:E_wlog3,def:quasiperiodic_diverging} we see that each $x_i$ must be a directed automorphism in $D = \Sigma\cap \DirTd$ since their portrait depth is more than $\ell$.

From the above factorisation, we construct our table $\tau_\mathrm{up}$ as follows.

\medskip
\noindent\textit{3.1.~Case 1: $\llbracket \zeta,p;\zeta',p'\rrbracket$}.

\smallskip\noindent
Let $\zeta = uv^\omega,\zeta = u'(v')^\omega$ where $(u,v),(u',v') \in I$, and let $p,p'\in C^{2\ell}$ be paths in the tree.
We then define  $\llbracket \zeta,p;\zeta',p'\rrbracket\cdot\tau_\mathrm{up}$ such that it contains all words of the form
\begin{multline*}
        \llbracket \zeta, v p_1 ; \alpha_1, q_1 \rrbracket 
        x_1
        \llbracket \alpha_1', q_1' ; \alpha_2, q_2 \rrbracket
        x_2
        \llbracket \alpha_2', q_2' ; \alpha_3, q_3 \rrbracket
        \\\cdots
        x_m
        \llbracket \alpha_k', q_k' ; \zeta', v' p_1' \rrbracket
    \in
    (\llbracket \zeta,p;\zeta',p'\rrbracket\cdot\tau_\mathrm{up})
\end{multline*}
where 
\begin{enumerate}
\item\label{item:main-therem/3.1/1}
$v p_1\in C^{2\ell}$ is the length-$2\ell$ prefix of $vp\in C^{3\ell}$;
\item\label{item:main-therem/3.1/2}
$v' p_1' \in C^{2\ell}$ is the length-$2\ell$ prefix of $v' p' \in C^{3\ell}$;
\item\label{item:main-therem/3.1/3}
each $\alpha_i = \mathrm{spine}(x_i) = u_i(v_i)^\omega$ where $(u_i, v_i) \in I$ and $q_i \in C^{2\ell}$;
\item \label{item:main-therem/3.1/4}
each $\alpha_i' = \mathrm{spine}(x_i)\acts x_i = u_i'(v_i')^\omega$ where $(u_i', v_i') \in I$ and $q_i' \in C^{2\ell}$; and
\item\label{item:main-therem/3.1/5}
there is a sequence of words $y_0,y_1,...,y_k \in C^\ell$ such that
\begin{itemize}
    \item $y_0$ is the length-$\ell$ suffix of $p\in C^{2\ell}$,
    \item $y_k$ is the length-$\ell$ suffix of $p'\in C^{2\ell}$,
\end{itemize}
and
\[
    (u_i v_i q_i y_{i-1})\acts x_i = u_i' v_i' q_i' y_i
\]
 for each $i \in \{1,2,...,k\}$ such that
\[
    4\ell=|u_i v_i q_i|
    <
    \CompDepthfin (u_i v_i q_iy_{i-1} , x_i) \leq |u_i v_i q_iy_{i-1}|=5\ell
\]
for each $i\in \{1,2,...,k\}$.
\end{enumerate}
Notice that item~\ref{item:main-therem/3.1/5}, as above, ensures that the action of the word is correct and that the letters $x_i$ are within $\ell$ of the maximum portrait depth that this symbol may represent.
In particular, items (\ref{item:main-therem/3.1/3},\ref{item:main-therem/3.1/4},\ref{item:main-therem/3.1/5}), as above, imply that for each $m\geq 0$, we have
\[
    (u_i (v_i)^m q_i y_{i-1})\acts x_i
    =
    u_i' (v_i')^m q_i' y_i
\]
 for each $i\in \{1,2,...,k\}$ such that
\[
    (m+3)\ell=|u_i (v_i)^m q_i|
    <
    \CompDepthfin (u_i (v_i)^m q_i y_{i-1} , x_i) \leq (m+4)\ell.
\]
for each $i\in \{1,2,...,k\}$.

\medskip
\noindent\textit{3.2.~Case 2: $\llbracket \zeta,p;\neg\eta\rrbracket$}.

\smallskip\noindent
Let $\zeta = u v^\omega$ where $(u,v) \in I$, and let $p\in C^{2\ell}$ be a path in the tree.
We then define $\llbracket \zeta,p;\neg\eta\rrbracket\cdot\tau_\mathrm{up}$ such that it contains all words of the form
\begin{multline*}
        \llbracket \zeta, v p_1 ; \alpha_1, q_1 \rrbracket 
        x_1
        \llbracket \alpha_1', q_1' ; \alpha_2, q_2 \rrbracket
        x_2
        \llbracket \alpha_2', q_2' ; \alpha_3, q_3 \rrbracket
        \\
        x_3
        \llbracket \alpha_3', q_3' ; \alpha_4, q_4 \rrbracket
        \cdots
        x_m
        \llbracket \alpha_k', q_k' ; \underline{\varphi} \rrbracket
    \in
    (\llbracket \zeta,p;\neg \eta\rrbracket\cdot\tau_\mathrm{up})
\end{multline*}
where
\begin{enumerate}
\item \label{item:main-therem/4.2/1}
$v p_1\in C^{2\ell}$ is the length-$2\ell$ prefix of $vp\in C^{3\ell}$;
\item\label{item:main-therem/4.2/2}
each $\alpha_i = \mathrm{spine}(x_i) = u_i (v_i)^\omega$ where $(u_i, v_i) \in I$, and $q_i \in C^{2\ell}$;
\item \label{item:main-therem/4.2/3}
each $\alpha_i' = \mathrm{spine}(x_i) \acts x_i = u_i' (v_i')^\omega$ where $(u_i', v_i') \in I$, and $q_i' \in C^{2\ell}$;
\item\label{item:main-therem/4.2/4}
there is a sequence of words $y_0,y_1,...,y_k \in C^\ell$ defined such that
\begin{itemize}
    \item $y_0$ is the length-$\ell$ suffix of $p\in C^{2\ell}$,
\end{itemize}
and
\[
    (u_i v_i q_i y_{i-1})\acts x_i
    =
    u_i' v_i' q_i' y_i
\]
for each $i\in \{1,2,...,k\}$ such that
\[
    4\ell=|u_i v_i q_i|
    <
    \CompDepthfin (u_i v_i q_i y_{i-1} , x_i) \leq |u_i v_i q_i y_{i-1}|=5\ell.
\]
for each $i\in \{1,2,...,k\}$; and
\item\label{item:main-therem/4.2/5}
the value of $\underline{\varphi}$ depends on the value of $y_k$, as in item~\ref{item:main-therem/4.2/4}, in particular,
\[
    \underline{\varphi}
    =
    \begin{cases}
        \neg\eta &\text{if }y_k = b\text{ where }\eta=ab^\omega \text{ with } (a,b)\in I\\
        \mathrm{any}&\text{otherwise}.
    \end{cases}
\]
\end{enumerate}
Notice that items~\ref{item:main-therem/4.2/4} and~\ref{item:main-therem/4.2/5}, as above, ensure that the action of the word does not stabilise the ray $\eta$, and that each letter $x_i$ have a portrait depth which is within $\ell$ of the maximum.
In particular, items~(\ref{item:main-therem/4.2/2},\ref{item:main-therem/4.2/3},\ref{item:main-therem/4.2/4}), as above, imply that for each $m\geq 0$, we have
\[
    (u_i (v_i)^m q_i y_{i-1})\acts x_i
    =
    u_i' (v_i')^m q_i' y_i
\]
for each $i\in \{1,2,...,k\}$ such that
\[
    (m+3)\ell=|u_i (v_i)^m q_i|
    <
    \CompDepthfin (u_i (v_i)^m q_i y_{i-1} , x_i) \leq (m+4)\ell.
\]
for each $i\in \{1,2,...,k\}$.

\medskip
\noindent\textit{3.3.~Case 3: $\llbracket \zeta,p;\mathrm{any}\rrbracket$}.

\smallskip\noindent
Let $\zeta = uv^\omega$ where $(u,v)\in I$, and let $p\in C^{2\ell}$ be a path in the tree.
We then define $\llbracket \zeta,p;\mathrm{any}\rrbracket\cdot\tau_\mathrm{up}$ such that it contains all words of the form
\begin{multline*}
        \llbracket \zeta, v p_1 ; \alpha_1, q_1 \rrbracket 
        x_1
        \llbracket \alpha_1', q_1' ; \alpha_2, q_2 \rrbracket
        x_2
        \llbracket \alpha_2', q_2' ; \alpha_3, q_3 \rrbracket
        \\\cdots
        x_m
        \llbracket \alpha_k', q_k' ; \mathrm{any} \rrbracket
    \in
    (\llbracket \zeta,p;\mathrm{any}\rrbracket\cdot\tau_\mathrm{up})
\end{multline*}
where
\begin{enumerate}
\item \label{item:main-therem/4.3/1}
$v p_1\in C^{2\ell}$ is the length-$2\ell$ prefix of $vp\in C^{3\ell}$;
\item\label{item:main-therem/4.3/2}
each with $\alpha_i = \mathrm{spine}(x_i) = u_i (v_i)^\omega$ where $(u_i, v_i) \in I$, and $q_i \in C^{2\ell}$; 
\item\label{item:main-therem/4.3/3} 
each with $\alpha_i' = \mathrm{spine}(x_i)\acts x_i = u_i' (v_i')^\omega$ where $(u_i', v_i') \in I$, and $q_i' \in C^{2\ell}$; and
\item\label{item:main-therem/4.3/4}
there is a sequence of paths $y_0,y_1,...,y_k \in C^\ell$ defined such that
\begin{itemize}
    \item $y_0$ is the length-$\ell$ suffix of $p\in C^{2\ell}$,
\end{itemize}
and
\[
    (u_i v_i q_i y_{i-1})\acts x_i
    =
    u_i' v_i' q_i' y_i
\]
for each $i\in \{1,2,...,k\}$ such that
\[
    4\ell=|u_i v_i q_i|
    <
    \CompDepthfin (u_i v_i q_i y_{i-1} , x_i) \leq |u_i v_i q_iy_{i-1}|=5\ell.
\]
for each $i\in \{1,2,...,k\}$.
\end{enumerate}
Notice that items~(\ref{item:main-therem/4.3/2},\ref{item:main-therem/4.3/3},\ref{item:main-therem/4.3/4}), as above, imply that for each $m\geq 0$, we have
\[
    (u_i (v_i)^m q_i y_{i-1})\acts x_i
    =
    u_i' (v_i')^m q_i' y_i
\]
for each $i\in \{1,2,...,k\}$ such that
\[
    (m+3)\ell=|u_i (v_i)^m q_i|
    <
    \CompDepthfin (u_i (v_i)^m q_i y_{i-1} , x_i) \leq (m+4)\ell.
\]
for each $i\in \{1,2,...,k\}$.

\medskip
\noindent\textit{3.4.~Computability.}

\smallskip\noindent
Notice that given the set $I\subset C^*\times C^*$ and the set of nonterminals, there is an algorithm which generates the finite state automata described in sections~3.1-3 of this proof.

\medskip
\noindent\textit{\underline{4.~Final table $\tau_\mathrm{finish}$}}.

\smallskip\noindent
Let $(a,b)\in I$ be such that $\eta = a b^\omega$.
For each $\zeta = u v^\omega, \zeta' = u' (v')^\omega$ where $(u,v),(u',v')\in I$ and each path $p,p'\in C^{2\ell}$, we define the table $\tau_\mathrm{finish}$ as
\begin{align*}
    (\llbracket \zeta,p ; \zeta',p'\rrbracket\cdot\tau_\mathrm{finish} )
    &=
    \left\{
        w \in \Sigma^*
    \ \middle|\
    \begin{aligned}
        (up)\acts w=u'p'
        \text{ and }\\
        \Decoratefin(up, x) = w_1^{(a_1)} w_2^{(a_2)}\cdots w_k^{(a_k)}\\
        \text{where each }a_i\leq |up|
    \end{aligned}
    \right\},
    \\
    (\llbracket \zeta,p ; \neg\eta\rrbracket \cdot \tau_\mathrm{finish})
    &=
    \left\{
        w \in \Sigma^*
    \ \middle|\
    \begin{aligned}
        (up)\acts w\neq a (b)^2
        \text{ and }\\
        \Decoratefin(up, x) = w_1^{(a_1)} w_2^{(a_2)}\cdots w_k^{(a_k)}\\
        \text{where each }a_i\leq |up|
    \end{aligned}
    \right\},
    \\
    (\llbracket \zeta,p ; \mathrm{any}\rrbracket \cdot \tau_\mathrm{finish})
    &=
    \left\{
        w \in \Sigma^*
    \ \middle|\
    \begin{aligned}
        \Decoratefin(up, x) = w_1^{(a_1)} w_2^{(a_2)}\cdots w_k^{(a_k)}\\
        \text{where each }a_i\leq |up|
    \end{aligned}
    \right\}.
\end{align*}
Notice that each of the above is a regular language, in particular, they can be recognised by a finite-state automaton with states of the form $q\in C^{3\ell}$, and thus, these automata have at most $d^{3\ell}$ states where $3\ell = |up|$.

\medskip
\noindent\textit{\underline{5.~Conclusion}}.

\smallskip\noindent
We see from our construction that
\[
    w \in \Sigma^* \cap (S\cdot\tau_\mathrm{init} (\tau_\mathrm{up})^m \tau_\mathrm{finish})
\]
if and only if both $w \in \WP(G,X,\Stab(1))$ and
\[
    \Decorate(\eta,w)
    =
    w_1^{(a_1)} w_2^{(a_2)} \cdots w_k^{(a_k)}
\]
where each $a_i \in \{0,1,2,...,(m+3)\ell\}\cup \{\infty\}$.
In particular, this is clear from the properties of the nonterminals as mentioned in section~1 of this proof.

Notice that for each $w\in \WP(G,X,\Stab(\eta))$, such an $m$ exists.
Moreover, we see that with each application of the tables $\tau_\mathrm{init}$, $\tau_\mathrm{up}$, $\tau_\mathrm{finish}$, we uniquely factor the word $w$ into finitely many subwords.
That is, each word generated by our grammar is generated unambiguously for each $m$.
We thus see that our grammar $E$ satisfies the requirements of an unambiguous limiting grammar as defined in \cref{def:unambiguous-et0l}.
The same statements hold for the grammar $E'$ and the language $\Sigma^*\setminus \WP(G,X,\Stab(\eta))$.
\end{proof}

\TheoremMainCor

\begin{proof}
    This follows from \cref{thm:main,lem:ET0L-membership-uniformly-decidable}.
\end{proof}

\section{Are stabilisers of infinite rays context-free?}\label{sec:not-CF} 

Since context-free languages are ET0L, it is natural to ask if \cref{thm:main} can be sharpened to context-free rather than ET0L. Given a bounded automata group $G\leq \AutTd$ with a finite generating set $X$ and $\eta$ any infinite ray in $\Td$, we provide two obstructions to the language $\WP(G,X,\Stab(\eta))$ being context-free. Exploiting results and techniques from \cite{BDN2017}, for a large class of groups we show that almost all of these languages are not context-free. Moreover, for a few key examples, we show that all such languages are not context-free. Hence, \cref{thm:main} cannot be improved to context-free languages. 

Recall that a group $G\leq \AutTd$ has an induced action on the boundary of the rooted regular tree. We can thus consider the family of Schreier graphs associated to this action, as in \cref{sec:introduction}.

Recall also that $\WP(G,X,\Stab(\eta))$ is the language of words which label closed paths from $\eta$ to $\eta$ in $\Gamma_\eta$, that is, the Schreier graph of $\Stab(\eta)$.
We notice that $\Gamma_\eta$ is a rooted graph.

\begin{definition}
Let $\Gamma_{v_{0}}$ be a graph rooted at $v_{0}$ and let $w$ be a vertex of $\Gamma_{v_{0}}$.
The \emph{end-cone} $\Gamma_{v_{0}} (v)$ is the connected component of $\Gamma_{v_{0}} \setminus B_{\Gamma_{v_{0}}}(v_{0},|v|)$ which contains $v$, where $B_{\Gamma_{v_{0}}}(v_{0},k)$ denotes the ball of radius $k$ centred at $v_{0}$ and $|v|$ is the distance of $v$ from $v_{0}$.
We denote with $\Delta_{v_{0}} (v)$ the set of vertices of $\Gamma_{v_{0}} (v)$ which are of minimal distance to $v_{0}$ and we call them \emph{frontier points} of the end-cone.    
\end{definition}

We say that two end-cones $\Gamma_{v_{0}} (v_1)$ and $\Gamma_{v_{0}} (v_2)$ \emph{have the same type} if there exists a graph isomorphism $\varphi\colon \Gamma_{v_{0}} (v_1)\to\Gamma_{v_{0}} (v_2)$ between them which respects the labelling and for which $\varphi(\Delta_{v_{0}}(v_1))=\Delta_{v_{0}} (v_2)$.
From this, we may then define a context-free graph as follows.

\begin{definition}
 A rooted graph is context-free if it has finitely many types of end-cones.
\end{definition}

By combining Proposition~7~in \cite{Rodaro2023}, which establishes an equivalence between the different notions of context-freeness in graphs, and Proposition~9~in~\cite{Rodaro2023}, which says that a context-free graph is quasi-isometric to a tree, we directly deduce our first criterion. 
\begin{theorem}[see \cite{Rodaro2023}]\label{thm:Sch-not-CF}
Let $G$ be a finitely generated bounded automata group and let $\eta$ be an infinite ray in $\partial \Td$. If $\WP(G,X,\Stab(\eta))$ is context-free, then the Schreier graph $\Gamma_\eta$ is quasi-isometric to a tree.
\end{theorem}

We denote by $\mu$ the uniform measure on the boundary of $\Td$. From now on, the terms ``almost surely'' and ``almost all'' are meant with respect to $\mu$.
An exhaustive study of the ends of Schreier graphs
in the case of self-similar bounded automata groups was completed in \cite{BDN2017}. We know that for a self-similar bounded automata group its Schreier graphs almost surely have one or two ends (Corollary~5~in~\cite{BDN2017}).
For the one-ended case, we have the following remark. 
\begin{remark}\label{remarco}
Recall that the number of ends is a quasi-isometric invariant. Also, it is straightforward that a one-ended tree is quasi-isometric to either the half line or the half line with infinitely many finite paths of unbounded lengths attached. For the first option, we will prove that a self-similar bounded automata group which is level-transitive cannot have more than two Schreier graphs quasi-isometric to a half-line. For the second option, it is not hard to see that the graph does not have finitely many end-cone types. 
\end{remark}

 In what follows we will heavily use results and methods developed in \cite{Bondarenko2007thesis, BDN2017}. We stress the fact that the machinery used here needs a stricter notion of boundness, in fact, we also require that our automata groups are self-similar.

 %%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%
The action of $G$ on $C^{n}$ gives rise to a finite graph $\Gamma^{n}$, where two words $x_{1}\cdots x_{n}$ and $y_{1}\cdots y_{n}$ are connected by an edge if there exists an element of $G$ mapping one to another. We denote with $\Gamma^{n}_{x_{1}\cdots x_{n}}$ the graph $\Gamma^n$ rooted at $x_{1}\cdots x_{n}$. We recall that the space of all such rooted graphs is equipped with the Gromov-Hausdorff convergence. In our case, given $\xi=x_1x_2\cdots$ we have that the sequence of rooted graphs $\Gamma^n_{x_1\cdots x_n}$ converges to $\Gamma_{\xi}$. This means that the ball $B_{\Gamma_{\xi}}(\xi, r)$ centred at $\xi$ of radius $r$ in $\Gamma_{\xi}$ is isomorphic to the ball $B_{\Gamma^n}(x_1\cdots x_n, r)$ centred at $x_1\cdots x_n$ of radius $r$ in $\Gamma^{n}$, for any $n$ large enough.


 %%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%

\begin{theorem} \label{thm: Sch-not-half-line}
Let $G$ be a finitely generated self-similar bounded automata group acting transitively on $C^n$, then the set 
\[
B\coloneqq\{\eta\mid \Gamma_\eta \textrm{ is quasi-isometric to a half-line}\}
\] 
consists of at most two orbits.    
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Suppose, by contradiction, that there are at least three Schreier graphs quasi-isometric to a half-line, say corresponding to the orbits of $\xi,\eta, \phi$ and let $\xi=x_1x_2\cdots$. 

If $R(\xi)$ is an infinite ray in $\Gamma_{\xi}$ starting from $\xi$, then by the very definition of quasi-isometry every vertex of $\Gamma_{\xi}$ not belonging to such a ray must have a bounded distance to it. Let $R(\xi)_n$ denote the vertex in $\Gamma^n$ which corresponds to the $n$-th vertex of $R(\xi)$. 
By our assumption, either $\eta$ or $\phi$ has prefixes whose distances from both $x_1\cdots x_n$ and $R(\xi)_n$ grow with $n$. Indeed, for both  $\eta$ and $\phi$, the distances among their prefixes and the prefixes of $\xi$ must diverge as they do not belong to the same orbit. Moreover, suppose that the prefixes of $\phi$ remain at a bounded distance from the $R(\xi)_n$'s for any $n$, then the fact that the prefixes of $\eta$ have an unbounded distance sequence to them follows from a triangular inequality and the fact that the prefixes of $\eta$ and $\phi$ are diverging with respect to the distance. Set $\eta=y_1y_2\cdots $ and recall that 
$\Gamma_{\eta}$ is quasi-isometric to a half-line. From what was said before, $d(R(\xi)_n, y_1\cdots y_n)\to \infty$ and $d(x_1\cdots x_n, y_1\cdots y_n)\to \infty$, for $n\to \infty$. Here $d(-,-)$ denotes the geodesic distance in the graph $\Gamma^n$ or in $\Gamma_{\xi}$. 

Consider the vertices $z_s:=y_1\cdots y_s x_{s+1} x_{s+2}\cdots $, for every $s\in \mathbb{N}$, i.e.\@ words that start with a prefix of $\eta$ and end with an infinite suffix of $\xi$. Note that $z_s\in \Gamma_{\xi}$ for every $s\geq 1$, because $\xi$ and the $z_s$'s are cofinal (see \cite{BDN2017}). By the Gromov-Hausdorff convergence $B_{\Gamma_{\eta}}(\eta, r)$ is isomorphic to $B_{\Gamma^{n}}(y_1\cdots y_n, r)$ for $n$ large enough, and such a ball is also isomorphic to $B_{\Gamma_{\xi}}(z_n , r)$. This means that $d(z_n,\xi)= d(y_1\cdots y_n, x_1\cdots x_n)$ and $d(z_n,R(\xi))= d(y_1\cdots y_n,R(\xi)_{n})$, so both $d(z_n,\xi)$ and $d(z_n,R(\xi))$ go to infinity.

To summarise, the sequence of $z_n$'s is diverging from $\xi$ and it is not at a bounded distance from $R(\xi)$. But this implies that $\Gamma_{\xi}$ has at least two ends, which is a contradiction.
 \end{proof}

On the other hand, \cite{BDN2017} provides a characterisation of the self-similar bounded automata groups for which almost all Schreier graphs have two ends. In the binary case, such automata generate groups whose nuclei are given by the automata defined in \cite{Sunic2007}. 
In particular, they include the first Grigorchuk group.  For all such groups, \cref{thm:Sch-not-CF} is not useful, because all the Schreier graphs are quasi-isometric to lines. For this reason, we will provide \cref{prop:torsion-not-CF} which applies to any bounded automata group which is torsion. To start, we need the following lemma whose proof is based on standard arguments. 

\begin{lemma}\label{lem:regular-geodesics}
Let $G$ be a finitely generated bounded automata group and let $\eta$ be an infinite ray in $\partial \Td$. If the Schreier graph $\Gamma_\eta$ is context-free, then the set of words one can read on geodesics in the graph starting from $\eta$ is a regular language.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}
We recall that, by definition, $\Gamma_\eta$ has finitely many end-cone types. All we have to do is to construct a finite state automaton which reads geodesics. The states are of the form $(C,\zeta)$ where $C$ is an end-cone type and $\zeta$ ranges in the frontier points of a given end-cone of type $C$. Note that the set of states is finite since the end-cone types are finite and the number of frontier points for a given end-cone is finite.
The initial state is $(C_{0},\eta)$ with $C_{0}$ the end-cone type related to the base vertex $\eta$ and all the states are finals. We then add a transition $(C_1,\zeta_1)\xrightarrow{a} (C_2,\zeta_2)$ if there exists an edge, labelled with $a$, from the frontier point corresponding to $\zeta_1$ of an end-cone of type $C_1$, to a point that corresponds to $\zeta_2$ which belongs to $\Gamma_\eta (\zeta_1) \setminus \Delta_{\eta} (\zeta_1)$.
Moreover, this vertex $\zeta_2$ belongs to $\Delta_\eta (\zeta_3)$ of an end-cone of type $C_2$. 
\end{proof}

\begin{proposition}\label{prop:torsion-not-CF}
Let $G$ be an infinite torsion bounded automata group which is finitely generated and let $\eta$ be an infinite ray. If the Schreier graph of $\eta$ is infinite, then $\WP(G,X,\Stab(\eta))$ is not context-free.
\end{proposition}
\begin{proof}
From Proposition~7~in~\cite{Rodaro2023}, we know that if $\WP(G,X,\Stab(\eta))$ is context-free, then so is the corresponding Schreier graph. Thus, from \cref{lem:regular-geodesics} we have that the language of all geodesics in this graph is regular.
Now, exploiting the pumping lemma of regular languages (see e.g.\@ \cite[Theorem 1.70]{S2013}), we see that this language of geodesics contains some language $\{xy^nz\mid n\in \mathbb N \}$ as a subset where $y \in X^*$ is a non-empty word.
This contradicts our assumption that $G$ is torsion, since $xy^kz$ cannot be a geodesic when $k$ is the order of the element given by $y$.
\end{proof}

To summarise, combining \cref{thm:Sch-not-CF} with \cref{thm: Sch-not-half-line} we show that there are finitely generated bounded automata groups such that almost surely the Schreier graphs are not context-free. An interesting example of a group which Schreier graphs are almost all one-ended trees, but not context-free, is the following.

\medskip
\noindent\textit{\underline{Iterated monodromy group of $z^2+i$}} (see \cite{BDN2017}). In self-similar form, it is generated by the three automorphisms
$$ a=(b,c),\ \ b=(1,1) \cdot s,\ \ c=(a,1)$$
where $1 \neq s \in \mathrm{Sym}(\{0,1\})$.
In this case it is easy to see that the trees are not quasi-isometric to half-lines. Indeed, 
if $\eta=c_1c_2\cdots$ is an infinite ray, the graph $\Gamma_{\eta}$ contains vertices $v_k:=0^kc_{k+1}\cdots$. By using an inductive argument, one can show that from each $v_k$ issues one path to $1^kc_{k+1}\cdots$ and a second different path to $1^{k-2}00c_{k+1}\cdots$. In particular, if the graph is one-ended, then it is a half-line with infinitely many paths of unbounded lengths attached, hence it is not context-free (see also \cref{remarco}).

\medskip

The main result of this paper, namely \cref{thm:main}, addresses a family of infinite rays which has measure zero. Thus far, a context-free version of such theorem could still be possible. For this reason, we will use \cref{thm:Sch-not-CF} and \cref{prop:torsion-not-CF} to provide some examples in which all the Schreier graphs are not context-free. Recall that if the Schreier graph of an infinite ray $\eta$ is not quasi-isometric to a tree, then $\WP(G,X,\Stab(\eta))$ is not context-free.

\medskip
\noindent\textit{\underline{Hanoi tower group on three pegs}} (see \cite{GS2006}). It is known that any Schreier graph of this group is one-ended and not quasi-isometric to the half-line (e.g.~Remark~3~in~\cite{BDN2017}), hence any $\WP(G,X,\Stab(\eta))$ is not context-free.
In fact, Theorem~11~in~\cite{BDN2017} provides a criterion to determine whether all the Schreier graphs are one-ended.

\medskip
\noindent\textit{\underline{Basilica group}}.
We can directly apply the theorem to show that the language $\WP(G,X,\Stab(\eta))$ cannot be context-free for the Basilica group, since
its Schreier graphs are fully classified in \cite{DDMN2010}. Namely, all Schreier graphs are one-ended, two-ended or four-ended. In fact, the latter case is a single exception.
Moreover, if the Schreier graph has one or two ends, by the classification it is not quasi-isometric to a tree. 
On the other hand, for the case of the four-ended graph, it is clear that it does not have finitely many end-cone types (see \cite[Theorem 4.6 and Figure 7]{DDMN2010}).

\medskip
\noindent\textit{\underline{Grigorchuk group}}. This example has almost all two-ended graphs. Namely, it has just 1 one-ended Schreier graph, which is the one containing $R^{\infty}$, the rightmost point in the boundary. All the other graphs have two ends. 
In this case, we use \cref{prop:torsion-not-CF} to prove the non-context-freeness.

\medskip

We end the section with a trivial example in which we can strengthen our result to context-free and a non-trivial example where we do not know whether $\WP(G,X,\Stab(\eta))$ is context-free.


\medskip
\noindent\textit{\underline{Infinite dihedral group}}. This group can too be seen as a bounded automata group (see \cref{fig:dihedral-automaton}).
One Schreier graph is one-ended (containing $R^{\infty}$), while all the others are isomorphic to the Cayley graph of $\mathbb{Z}$ over a standard generator. Suppose that the generators of the infinite dihedral group are $a$ and $b$, then the two isomorphism classes of Schreier graphs are labelled as in \cref{fig:dihedral-schreier}. It is easy to see that the Schreier graphs are all context-free.

