\section{Further Research} \label{sec:future}

Even though it is well-known that any language can be seen as an infinite intersection of regular ones, we give this characterisation of the word problem which may be useful.

First, we observe the following fact.
\begin{remark}
If $w \in \WP(G,X,\Stab(\eta))$, then $w$ stabilises all the prefixes of $\eta$.
\end{remark}

Now, we can express the word problem with respect to the languages $\WP(G,X,\Stab(\eta))$.
\begin{lemma}\label{lem:stab-wp}
The word problem of a self-similar group coincides with the intersection of all $\WP(G,X,\Stab(\eta))$ with $\eta$ periodic.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
It is straightforward that any $\WP(G,X,\Stab(\eta))$ contains the word problem. On the other side, the action on $\mathcal{T}_{d}$ is faithful. So, if an element stabilises all the vertices of the tree, then it is the identity. Take a word $w$ in the intersection of all $\WP(G,X,\Stab(\eta))$ with $\eta$ periodic. This means that $w$ stabilises all possible finite words and hence it is the identity.
\end{proof}


The proof can also be done by using a topological argument. Consider the action of the group onto $\partial \mathcal{T}_d$. The set of periodic infinite words is dense in the boundary. So if a word fixes this set, then it fixes the whole boundary. Hence, the word belongs to the word problem by faithfulness.

By simply combining \cref{lem:stab-wp} with \cref{thm:main}, we have the following result.
\begin{proposition}
The word problem of a bounded automata group is an intersection of ET0L languages.
\end{proposition}


This motivates a further investigation on the topic. In particular, we ask the following.
\begin{question}
Is it true that the word problem of a bounded automata group is a finite intersection of ET0L languages?
\end{question}

In \cref{sec:not-CF}, we prove that the languages $\WP(G,X,\Stab(\eta))$ are not context-free under some additional hypothesis. Moreover, we exhibit an example for which we do not know whether the languages are context-free. 

\begin{question}
Does it exist a non-trivial example of a self-similar bounded automata group with $\WP(G,X,\Stab(\eta))$ context-free?
\end{question}


The case in which $\eta$ is non-periodic or non-computable is yet to be understood. For this reason, it would be interesting to answer the following questions.

\begin{question}
Is it true that for all non-computable words $\eta$ the language $\WP(G,X,\Stab(\eta))$ is non-computable? 
\end{question}

On the other hand, we are interested to know if $\eta$ is computable from $\WP(G,X,\Stab(\eta))$. That is, if the language uniquely identifies the same word.

\begin{question}
Let  $\eta$ be the word $0101^2 01^3 01^4 \ldots$. Is $\WP(G,X,\Stab(\eta))$ not ET0L for the Grigorchuk group?
\end{question}

Notice that by combining \cref{thm:gfun,thm:main}, we have a characterisation of the generating function for $\WP(G,X,\Stab(\eta))$, the languages of closed loops where $\eta$ is eventually periodic.
There are certain subclasses of \emph{indexed languages} which have known characterisations of their generating functions for which it is easier to find some closed-form expressions (see~\cite{adams2013}).
We would be interested in knowing if our results can be strengthened to such subclasses of indexed languages.

\begin{question}
    For what bounded automata group and infinite rays does the language $\WP(G,X,\Stab(\eta))$ belong to the subclasses of indexed languages as studied in~\cite{adams2013}?
\end{question}

