\section{Discussion}
\label{sec:discussion}

% We introduced SEZ-HARN - Self-Explainable Zero-shot Human Activity Recognition Network, building upon recent advancements in zero-shot human activity recognition (ZS-HAR) models such as VbZSL \cite{p_har_zsl_video} and TEZARNet \cite{125PathirageICONIP2023}. SEZ-HARN constructs the semantic space using auxiliary video data, leveraging the rich motion-specific information embedded in activity videos, which are both abundant and accessible. The key innovation of SEZ-HARN lies in its ability to generate self-explanatory skeleton movement videos, addressing the lack of explainability in existing ZS-HAR models \cite{p_har_zsl_wemb, p_har_zsl_mcross, p_har_zsl_video}. While prior supervised sensor-based HAR models \cite{p_xai_har,p_dexar,dubey2022xai} have incorporated post-hoc explanation methods such as SHAP \cite{p_shap} and Grad-CAM \cite{selvaraju2017grad}, these approaches are not specifically designed for zero-shot models and often produce explanations that are difficult for non-expert users to interpret. SEZ-HARN bridges this gap by generating visually intuitive skeleton movement videos, enabling transparent and user-friendly explanations.

% We evaluated SEZ-HARN on four publicly available IMU-based human activity datasets, comparing its performance in unseen class recognition against established black-box ZS-HAR models. As presented in Table~\ref{tab:comp}, SEZ-HARN consistently outperforms MLCLM and VbZSL across all datasets and achieves performance comparable to TEZARNet, except for the PAMAP2 dataset, where its accuracy is marginally lower. These results demonstrate that introducing self-explainability in SEZ-HARN does not compromise classification performance. Moreover, leveraging video data for semantic space construction and extracting temporal features from IMU data improves recognition accuracy for unseen activities.

% Besides the predictive performance, we assessed the quality of the explanatory skeleton movement videos generated by SEZ-HARN, focusing on two aspects: realism and interpretability. Realism was quantified using the Discrete Fréchet Distance, with results indicating high realism across datasets, except for UTD-MHAD. The lower realism in UTD-MHAD is likely attributable to its smaller sample size and limited variety of principal body movements. A complementary user study confirmed that the generated videos were perceived as intuitive and easily understandable. These findings support the potential of SEZ-HARN for practical deployment, offering accurate predictions alongside explanations that are accessible to lay users.

% Despite its strengths, SEZ-HARN inherits limitations from the underlying datasets, including restricted diversity of activities, class imbalance, and potential actor bias, which may constrain its generalizability. Future work can address these challenges by expanding dataset diversity and incorporating augmented auxiliary data to further enhance the model’s zero-shot learning capabilities.

% Future research directions include refining the explanation generation mechanism to emphasize salient motion patterns that influence model decisions, thereby improving user comprehension. Another promising avenue is developing interactive explanations that allow users to explore model predictions at varying levels of granularity and through multiple formats. Furthermore, enhancing SEZ-HARN’s scalability to accommodate a broader range of activity domains and real-world complexities will strengthen its applicability in dynamic and diverse human activity recognition scenarios.

We propose SEZ-HARN—Self-Explainable Zero-shot Human Activity Recognition Network—extending recent ZS-HAR models such as VbZSL \cite{p_har_zsl_video} and TEZARNet \cite{125PathirageICONIP2023}. SEZ-HARN constructs the semantic space using auxiliary activity videos, leveraging their rich motion information. A key innovation is its ability to generate self-explanatory skeleton movement videos, addressing the explainability limitations in existing ZS-HAR models \cite{p_har_zsl_wemb, p_har_zsl_mcross, p_har_zsl_video}. While prior supervised sensor-based HAR models \cite{p_xai_har,p_dexar,dubey2022xai} have incorporated post-hoc explanation methods such as SHAP \cite{p_shap} and Grad-CAM \cite{selvaraju2017grad}, these approaches are not specifically designed for zero-shot models and often produce explanations that are difficult for non-expert users to interpret. SEZ-HARN bridges this gap by generating visually intuitive skeleton movement videos, enabling transparent and user-friendly explanations.

We evaluated SEZ-HARN on four publicly available IMU-based HAR datasets. As shown in Table~\ref{tab:comp}, it consistently outperforms MLCLM and VbZSL, and achieves comparable accuracy to TEZARNet—except on PAMAP2, where performance is slightly lower. These results indicate that self-explainability in SEZ-HARN does not compromise recognition performance. Moreover, constructing the semantic space from video data and leveraging temporal features from IMU signals enhances recognition of unseen activities.

To assess explanation quality, we analyzed realism and interpretability of the generated skeleton videos. Realism, measured via Discrete Fréchet Distance, was high across datasets, with the exception of UTD-MHAD, likely due to its smaller sample size and limited motion diversity. A user study confirmed that the videos were perceived as intuitive and interpretable, supporting SEZ-HARN’s potential for real-world deployment.

Nevertheless, SEZ-HARN inherits dataset limitations such as limited activity diversity, class imbalance, and actor bias, which may affect generalizability. Future work should explore more diverse and augmented auxiliary data to strengthen zero-shot performance. Other future directions include improving the explanation mechanism to highlight salient motion patterns that influence model decisions, developing interactive and multi-modal explanations, and enhancing scalability to accommodate broader and more complex HAR domains.

\section{Conclusion}
\label{sec:conclusion}

We present a Zero-Shot Human Activity Recognition (ZS-HAR) model that addresses two key challenges for adapting IMU-based HAR models to real-world scenarios: the limited availability of labeled data and the lack of transparency in existing models. The proposed approach leverages video data to learn semantic relationships between seen and unseen classes while generating skeleton movement videos to explain its decisions. Extensive experiments on four benchmark datasets—PAMAP2, DiLiAc, UTD-MHAD, and MHEALTH—demonstrate that the model effectively captures the semantic alignment between seen and unseen classes, outperforming state-of-the-art ZS-HAR models in all datasets except PAMAP2. Furthermore, the generated explanations are both realistic and intuitive, ensuring they are easily understandable to human users.

