\section{Results and Discussions}

\subsection{MOS Scores on StepEval-Audio-360}
The evaluation results on the StepEval-Audio-360 benchmark reveal distinct performance profiles among the three models, as illustrated in Figure \ref{fig:radar}.

Step-Audio-AQAA demonstrates a leading edge across multiple key dimensions. Notably in Speech Emotion Control, Step-Audio-AQAA showed its superior ability in expressing and recognizing vocal emotions. In Creativity, Language Ability, Gaming and Role-playing, Step-Audio-AQAA also achieved the highest scores, indicating its comprehensive strength in understanding complex instructions, generating diverse content, and engaging in fluent interactions. For Logical Reasoning and Voice Understanding, Step-Audio-AQAA also led, although its advantage in the latter was relatively marginal.

Step-Audio-AQAA has certain disadvantages in the two dimensions of Singing and Voice Instruction Following. This is because adding excessive sing data to enable the model to learn singing will seriously damage other capabilities; meanwhile, the lack of data similar to Voice Instruction Following also leads to the model's weak performance in this capability. We will leave these optimizations for the future.


% In role-playing, language ability, gaming Step-Audio-AQAA significantly outperformed Qwen-Omni and Kimi-Audio. This demonstrates its strength in maintaining coherent dialogue flow and adapting to dynamic conversational contexts, reflecting its robustness in interactive tasks. DPO and Model Merge also contribute to making the multi-turn output of audio tokens more in line with human preferences.

% For creativity, logical reasoning, and voice understanding, Step-Audio-AQAA outperformed GLM-4-Voice but slightly lagged behind Step-Audio-AQTA. This suggests that Step-Audio-AQAA excels at generating imaginative and contextually appropriate audio responses, likely due to its interleaved output pattern, which generates audio tokens conditioned on textual tokens. This approach enhances semantic coherence and flexibility in open-ended tasks.

% Conversely, Step-Audio-AQTA excelled in singing, speech emotion control, and gaming. However, since it is limited to outputting textual tokens, its strong performance in these dimensions can be attributed to the complementary integration of Step-Audio-TTS-3B, a high-performance TTS model. This highlights the critical role of token-based vocoder in enhancing overall performance for AQAA tasks, as the quality and expressiveness of audio token generation significantly impact the model’s capabilities.

