\subsection{Gradient Computation}
Gradient-based methods rely on first-order derivatives of the loss function ($L$, where $L = \mathcal{L}(\theta)$) to identify which parameters ($\theta = \{\theta_1, \theta_2, \ldots, \theta_n\}$) have the strongest immediate effect on the loss~\cite{GradientVsHessian_theory}. We use PyTorch’s automatic differentiation \cite{pytorch_autograd_tutorial} to measure gradient norms of the model parameters. 
