\section{Methods}

\subsection{Multi-agent Simulation Framework}

A centralised multi-agent framework for agentic reasoning was introduced~\cite{trencsenyi2025approximating}, combining an Observe–Orient–Decide–Act (OODA) control loop~\cite{OODA_agents} with multi-level hypergames~\cite{Wang1988} to support recursive reasoning among agents. Here we define a streamlined variant that omits explicit evaluation of recursive reasoning and focuses on core agent interactions within our domain. We let $X$ denote the space of natural language game descriptions and $C$ the set of agent contexts. An \textit{umpire} $\upsilon$, conceptualised as a pseudo-player~\cite{Rasmusen2006introtogametheory}, initialises each agent with a context $c_i \in C$ and instruction model $m_i \in M$. The umpire further enables gameplay by pairing agents and managing their participation in the guessing game, denoted as $G^*$.

% Rewritten by KS:- \cite{trencsenyi2025approximating} introduced a centralized multi-agent framework which implements agents driven by an Observe-Orient-Decide-Act loop~\cite{OODA_agents} and relies on multi-level hypergames~\cite{Wang1988} to accommodate recursive reasoning agents. We define our tailored version -- omitting the explicit recursive reasoning evaluation component -- as follows. Given the space of natural language game descriptions $X$ and agent contexts $C$, the umpire $\upsilon$ is a pseudo-player~\cite{Rasmusen2006introtogametheory} that initializes each agent with a context $c_i \in C$, and instruction model type $M$ and facilitates games by requesting each pair of players to participate in the guessing game $G^*$. 

Players' internal processes are defined as the interpretation function $I: X\times C \times M\rightarrow\mathbb{Z}$ -- wrapping the agent cycle's orient and decision stages --, which we refer to as a cognitive process, producing a guess from a natural language game description, player context and instruction. Our experiments implement agents with different implementations of $I$.

The work in~\cite{trencsenyi2025approximating} entails a perspective-based formal definition of guessing games that captures individuals' levels of recursive thinking. Our 2-player guessing games in this context are defined as $G = (N,p,A,U)$, where $N=\{i,j\}$ are our two players, and $p=\frac{2}{3}$ is the target multiplier. The action space is $A = A_i = A_{-i} \subseteq \mathbb{Z}$, where $i$ and $-i$ are the player and their opponent, $A$ is a finite interval of integers shared by both players, such as $A = \{0, 1, \dotsc, 100\}$ and $U: A \rightarrow \mathbb{R}$ is the utility function, specified as Equation 1 below.
\begin{equation}
U_i(a_i, a_{-i}) = 
\begin{cases}
1 & \text{if } |a_i - p \mu| < |a_{-i} - p \mu| \\
0.5 & \text{if } |a_i - p \mu| = |a_{-i} - p \mu| \\
0 & \text{otherwise}
\end{cases}
\end{equation}
where $\mu = \frac{a_i + a_{-i}}{2}$.

In the following sections, we introduce a set of agents within the framework defined above. First, we consider a game-theoretic model as a simple benchmark agent, then present two LLM-driven artificial reasoners that implement $I$ with different levels of conceptual sophistication.

\subsection{Experience Weighted Attraction}

Camerer et al. introduced the self-tuning experience weighted attraction model (EWA) as a single free-parameter game-theoretic learning algorithm designed to produce results that mimic human behaviour~\cite{ho2007self}. We implement the self-tuning EWA as a benchmark, given that it is a well-established state-of-the-art model from the economics literature. The heart of the model is the attraction mechanism composed as follows.

\begin{equation}
    \text{Memory}^j_i(t) = \phi \cdot N(t-1) \cdot \mathcal{A}^j_i(t-1)
\end{equation}

The memory component defines how past experience is carried forward, composed of the experience weight $N$~\cite{CAMERER2002137} and the change detector function $\phi$, defined in equations 3 and 4 below.

\begin{equation}
    N(t) = (1-\kappa) \cdot \phi \cdot N(t-1)+1
\end{equation}

\begin{equation}
    \phi(t)=1-\frac{1}{2}S_i(t)
\end{equation}

The surprise index function is composed of the immediate change vector $h$ and cumulative change vector $r$:

\begin{equation}
    S_i(t)=\sum^{m_{-i}}_{k=1}{(h^k_i(t)-r^k_i(t))^2}    
\end{equation}
\begin{equation}
    h^k_i(t)=\frac{\sum^{t}_{\tau=1}{\mathcal{I}(s^k_{-i},s_{-i}(\tau))}}{t}
\end{equation}
\begin{equation}
    r^k_i(t)=\mathcal{I}(s^k_{-i},s_{-i}(t))
\end{equation}
where $\mathcal{I}$ is a binary index function.

The reinforcement component describes how new experiences are recorded:
\begin{equation}
    \text{Reinforcement}^j_i(t) = [\delta+(1-\delta) \cdot \mathcal{I}(s^j_i,s_i(t))] \cdot \pi_i(s^j_i,s_{-i}(t))
\end{equation}

The foregone payoffs are denoted by $\delta$:
\begin{equation}
    \delta_{ij}(t)= \begin{cases}
        1 & \text{if } \pi_i(s^j_i,s_{-i}(t)) \geq \pi_i(t),\\
        0 & \text{otherwise.}
    \end{cases}
\end{equation}

where $\pi_i(s^j_i,s_{-i}(t))$ is $i's$ payoff for selecting strategy $j$ against $s_{-i}$ at time $t$. Players' attractions are updated according to equation 10 below 

\begin{equation}
    \mathcal{A}^j_i(t) = \frac{\text{Memory}^j_i(t) + \text{Reinforcement}^j_i(t)}{N(t-1) \cdot \phi \cdot (1-\kappa)}
\end{equation}

where $\kappa=0$, and players choose a strategy according to the distribution $P^j_i(t+1) = \frac{e^{\lambda \cdot \mathcal{A}^j_i(t)}}{\sum^{m_i}_{k=1}{e^{\lambda \cdot \mathcal{A}^k_i(t)}}}$, where $\lambda$ is the single parameter that requires manual tuning and controls the sensitivity of the model. The initial attraction $\mathcal{A}^j_i(0)$ is implemented according to the cognitive hierarchy model and the Poisson distribution function $P(k) = \frac{e^{-\tau}\tau^k}{k!}$~\cite{camerer2004cognitive}, and we set $N(0)=1$. Finally, we adopt $\lambda=2.39$ and $\tau=1.5$, which are the reported optimal parameters, tested on n-player guessing games~\cite{CAMERER2002137}.

Given that the self-tuning EWA model does not process natural language, we integrate EWA with our framework by implementing $I_{\text{EWA}}:G \rightarrow \mathbb{Z}$, where $I_{\text{EWA}}(G) \sim P(t+1)$ denotes sampling from the induced probability distribution over the strategies. In addition, in the case of EWA players, the responsibilities of $\upsilon$ are extended by the translation function $T:X\rightarrow G$, and the subject of game requests is $G^*$ in place of the natural language description $X$.

\subsection{Artificial Reasoners}

%TODO: nice to have but no space: add a side-by-side plot, showing the holistic view vs. the simple/reasoner decision making process

In this section, we introduce 3 categories of agentic and conceptual sophistication of the implementation suitably adapted from~\cite{trencsenyi2025approximating}: (1) the LLM used for reasoning, (2) the model used for composing the LLM prompts and (3) the agentic workflow the LLM is wrapped in.

\paragraph{LLM}
We tested two state-of-the-art LLMs with our artificial reasoners~\cite{claude37sonnet} representing different levels of complexity: (1) Claude \textbf{Haiku} 3.5 is a quick and compact model with strong reasoning capabilities and (2) \textbf{Sonnet} 3.7 is the latest, most capable model, suitable for complex problem-solving.

\paragraph{Prompting Model}
We construct our prompts based on the typical separation: context ($C$) and instruction ($M$). Providing LLMs with a character description aids their reasoning and instruction following~\cite{park2023generativeagentsinteractivesimulacra,mao2024alympicsllmagentsmeet,trencsenyi2025approximating}. We define three context configurations that represent different, increasingly more elaborate details for the LLM-driven agent as $C=\{c_{\varnothing},c_{\text{sim}},c_{\text{bio}}\}$:
\begin{itemize}
    \item \textbf{No profile ($\mathbf{c_{\varnothing}}$):} agents are presented with situational descriptions and are instructed to pick a number without further context;
    \item \textbf{Simple profile ($\mathbf{c_{\text{sim}}}$):} agents are instructed to reason according to a role specification, whether the agent is an expert of game theory or a novice without any expertise;
    \item \textbf{Biography ($\mathbf{c_{\text{bio}}}$):} the Umpire initialises each player with an elaborate background supporting the specific role.
\end{itemize}

Furthermore, we implement an additional configuration parameter based on a psychological model of human decision-making inspired by the agentic model of appropriateness (\textbf{MoA}) from~\cite{vezhnevets2023generative}. We instruct our agents to address three questions in their responses: (1) \textit{What kind of situation is this?}, (2) \textit{What kind of person am I?} and (3)\textit{What should a person like me do in a situation like this?} While the decoupled decision-making capability specified in our multi-agent framework already implements subtasking, having the LLM respond via the three questions integrates an additional layer of chain-of-thought prompting aiding reasoning~\cite{wei2023chainofthoughtpromptingelicitsreasoning}. Furthermore, as the three questions are associated with how humans make decisions, and as LLM training data contains a vast amount of samples of human culture, we adopt the hypothesis that guiding the agent through the human-inspired patterns will support the generated reasoning to better approximate human responses. We define MoA as a binary feature encapsulated in the instruction $M=\{m_0,m_1\}$, where $m_1$ denotes the configuration that implements MoA.

\paragraph{Agentic Concept}

First, implementing the decoupled revision and decision mechanism from~\cite{trencsenyi2025approximating}, we define the \textbf{Reasoner} ($R$) agent as follows. The interpretation function $I:X \times C \times M \rightarrow \mathbb{Z}$ is defined as $I(x,c_i,m_i)=\delta(\rho(x,c_i,m_i), c_i, m_i)$, where
\begin{itemize}
    \item $\rho: X \times C \times M \rightarrow \Xi$ is the reasoning function: $\rho(x,c,m)=\xi_i$, where $\xi_i \in \Xi$ is $i$'s  belief about the opponent, a natural language reasoning based on game description $x$, context $c_i$, and instruction model $m_i$ on what the opponent guess $\hat{a}_{-i}$ is expected to be;
    \item $\delta : \Xi \times C \times M \rightarrow A$ is the decision function: $\delta(\xi_i,c_i,m_i)$, selecting $i$'s guess $a_i^*$ based on the belief $\xi_i$, context $c_i$, and instruction model $m_i$.
\end{itemize}

In contrast, the \textbf{Simple} ($S$) agent implements a one-step reasoning process, reflecting the growing association in recent literature between LLMs and agents through a looser definition of agency. Simple agents invoke \(I(x,c_i,m_i) = \delta'(x,c_i,m_i)\), where the decision function \(\delta': X \times C \times M \rightarrow A\) internally applies a (possibly identity) mapping \(f: X \rightarrow X'\), producing a belief-enriched description \(x' \in X' = X \times \Xi\). The resulting player guess \(a_i^*\) may thus rely either on the original game description or on an inferred belief \(\xi_{-i}\) about the opponent's guess.

\section{Experiments and Discussion}
%TODO: consider generating an appendix with additional plots and tables... means, k-levels, student/expert heatmaps + reasoning samples of interesting effects !

The following sections provide a detailed analysis of the experimental results, structured around multiple levels of behavioural fidelity.%\footnote{The source code and datasets are provided with the supplementary materials and will be made available in a public repository after publication.} 
We evaluate agents’ ability to replicate human-like behaviour at two levels of granularity: first, at the population level, assessing how closely agent outputs align with aggregated human behaviour; and second, at the subpopulation level, measuring how well agents distinguish and replicate the behavioural differences between distinct human groups (e.g., students vs. experts). Within each level, we analyze performance across complementary dimensions of behavioural alignment: (1) mean response behaviour via $k$-level approximation; (2) distributional similarity, using Wasserstein distance; and (3) frequency of zero guesses. Finally, we extend our evaluation to an out-of-sample setting by modifying key game parameters to assess how well agents generalise beyond likely training data priors. For each pairwise comparison where mentioned, we first test for variance homogeneity using Levene’s test. We apply either a standard independent samples t-test (equal variances) or Welch’s t-test (unequal variances). If the data is severely skewed (|skewness| > 2), we use the non-parametric Mann–Whitney U test instead.

\subsection{Benchmark Model Performance}

\begin{figure}[h]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\linewidth]{plots/human_vs_ewa_distributions.pdf}
\caption{Distribution of EWA over joint human results and k-levels.}
\label{fig:ewa}
\end{figure}

As an established game-theoretic model, we first compare the self-tuning EWA agent with the overall human guess, as shown in Figure~\ref{fig:ewa}. The EWA model outperforms human results, averaging around $k=3$. Descriptive statistics confirm this observation: human guesses have a mean of $29.05$ and a standard deviation of $28.28$, while EWA agent guesses average $11.19$ with a standard deviation of $2.50$. Levene's test indicates heterogeneous variances, and a Welch’s $t$-test confirms a statistically significant difference between the groups ($t = 10.80$, $p < 0.0001$). The Wasserstein distance between the two distributions is substantial ($W = 22.34$). The EWA agent produces no zero guesses, indicating that while the EWA model achieves highly strategic behaviour, it systematically diverges from human reasoning patterns, failing to replicate key empirical properties of human play.

\subsection{Artifical Reasoners}

In the following sections, we test each LLM-based agent configuration across Haiku and Sonnet models, agent types -- $S$ or $R$ -- and variations of $C$ and $M$. We refer to specific agent configurations such that, e.g. $S_{c_\varnothing,m_0}$ denotes the Simple agent with no profile, and no MoA or $R_{c_{bio},m_1}$ denotes the Reasoner agent with the Bio context using MoA. Given that EWA cannot process contextual information, we consider it implements $c_\varnothing,m_0$. The two main test cases are specified by testing the (1) overall human-likedness of agents and (2) the ability to approximate human guesses specific to roles.

\subsubsection{Population-wise Performance}

First, we consider the case where the players' individual details -- i.e. the personal profile defining whether the player is a student or an expert -- are not relevant. Data with subgroup representation is pooled together to represent the overall population. 

In order to estimate and discuss the reasoning sophistication of our different implementations, we convert the guess values to k-levels as follows. Assuming irrational $k=0$ actors act randomly and produce a mean of 50~\cite{camerer2004cognitive}, we solve $(\frac{2}{3})^k \cdot 50=a_i^*$ to obtain $i$'s reasoning level. We set one dimension as agentic sophistication, where the order of sophistication is set as $\text{EWA} < S < R$. The second dimension describes the sophistication of the implemented $C$ and $M$, such that 
\[
    c_\varnothing,m_0<c_\varnothing,m_1<c_{\text{sim}},m_0<c_{\text{sim}},m_1<c_{\text{bio}},m_0<c_{\text{bio}},m_1
\]
defines the order of sophistication. Our initial expectation is that there is a linear relation between the two axes of sophistication and the human-likedness of the results. In Figure~\ref{fig:heatmap1}, we present LLM-wise heatmaps of the absolute mean error (AME) $\left| \bar{k}_{\text{true}} - \bar{k}_{\text{pred}} \right|$ of the true human and the agent predicted k values. We can observe that the least complex implementation (EWA) showed the most distant performance, and Haiku produced the best results with the most complex implementation, which matches humans' reasoning level with a difference of just $0.02$. However, while the opposite ends of the complexity range matched our expectations, the intermediate results are not always consistent with the progressive inclusion of more sophisticated implementations. Sonnet shows the opposite reaction to more advanced configurations and, overall, is more distant from human reasoners than the simpler LLM. 

We transform our categorical features into ordinal variables as follows: agent $\in \{\text{EWA}=0, S=1, R=2\}$, $c \in \{c_{\varnothing}=0,c_{sim}=1,c_{bio}=2\}$, $m \in \{m_0=0 ,m_1=1\}$, and model $\in \{\text{Haiku}=0 , \text{Sonnet}=1\}$. Then, the overall sophistication score is computed as their sum. Table~\ref{tab:sophistication_effects} reveals the statistical effects of individual sophistication factors on our LLM-based agents, measured by linear regression models on the k-level AME. While the agent type does not significantly influence the results, the sophistication of LLM, $C$ and $M$ have significant effects. However, the effect direction is not aligned with our expectations, and a Spearman correlation test confirms a weak and insignificant negative correlation between the sophistication score and deviation from humans' k-level.

