\subsubsection{Subpopulation-wise Performance}
Simulations that require an individual-based approach where specific subpopulations can be represented explicitly must implement role specification. The following analysis concerns the configurations supporting profile assignment and investigates how well artificial agents can replicate the intergroup differences, role-specific reasoning levels, and guess distributions. Figure~\ref{fig:humans} depicting human results per cohort shows a higher density of guesses over lower values and a significantly lower mean of guesses for experts. In this section, we use those results as a benchmark and evaluate artificial agents' ability to replicate the significant difference in reasoning between human students and experts. 

First, we calculate the k-level means for students and experts separately and define $\Delta = \left| \bar{k}_{\text{true}}^{\text{student}} - \bar{k}_{\text{pred}}^{\text{student}} \right| - \left| \bar{k}_{\text{true}}^{\text{expert}} - \bar{k}_{\text{pred}}^{\text{expert}} \right|$ to measure if the agent replicates student and expert behaviour equally well/poorly, where values close to $0$ indicate an approximation of the difference between the human groups. Figure~\ref{fig:heatmap_profiles} entails the $\Delta$ heatmaps for Haiku and Sonnet. The dominantly negative values in the case of $S$ agents across both models indicate that these agents are consistently closer to expert behaviour, where likely the overestimation of student reasoning levels is due to an overfit to game-theoretic optimizations we observed in previous instances. On the other hand, $R$ agents show a more balanced behaviour and manage to approximate better the human differences with all $C \times M$ errors within $+/-0.4$ k levels. In addition, the transition from $m_0$ to $m_1$ consistently causes agents to optimise more and overestimate reasoning levels. The statistical significance test indicates lesser divergence in the case of $R$ agents using Haiku and the minor and insignificant differences in the case of $R_{c_{\text{sim}},m_0}$ and $R_{c_{\text{bio}},m_0}$ indicate closer matching of the human intergroup divergence.


