\section{Introduction}
\IEEEPARstart{U}{avs}, or drones, are increasingly being integrated into IoT ecosystems to support various applications, such as surveillance and monitoring, smart cities, and infrastructure inspection \cite{uav_iot_applications}. Their high mobility and rich sensing capabilities make them an ideal candidate for data collection, communication, and service delivery tasks in these applications. Designing UAVs that have intelligence to perform complex tasks in these applications correctly remains challenging, as it demands both robust instruction interpretation and executing tasks with robust reasoning. Recent advances in LLMs~\cite{GPT4, Llamma, Gemini, deepseek} have demonstrated remarkable proficiency in robotic areas such as planning~\cite{planning}, navigation~\cite{Navi, L3MVN}, communication~\cite{Survey}, and automation~\cite{automation}. With LLMs' exceptional natural language (NL) understanding and context generation capabilities, LLMs offer a promising avenue for robots with greater intelligence. Motivated by these developments, research efforts have been made towards the reliable integration of LLMs and UAVs~\cite{ChatGPTRobotics, TypeFly, CLEAR, AdaptiveRAG, GSCE}, to enable LLM-driven UAV operations.

Despite LLMs' potential for various robotic tasks, LLMs continue to face challenges from logical reasoning~\cite{logicalreasoning}, undermining their reliability in tasks that require complex decision-making. Crucially, unlike text generation applications where semantic-level accuracy is sufficient, UAV operation requires the precise execution of sequential actions. Erroneous or invalid control code can lead to unsafe UAV behavior and pose safety and security risks to the public, such as UAV crashes. Recent studies leverage prompt engineering~\cite{PromptEngineering} to enhance LLM in translating NL instructions into control code~\cite{ChatGPTRobotics,CodeasPolicies,GSCE}. However, these approaches adopt open-loop designs and lack mechanisms to verify the correctness of the synthesized code, leaving questions such as cumulative error and temporal action consistency unsolved~\cite{OpenLoopQuestions}.

