Inspired by control theory, more recent studies have adopted a closed-loop architecture that enables evaluation of generated code and provides feedback for refinement, thereby steering robot execution towards prescribed task objectives~\cite{AutoTAMP, ISR-LLM, CoPAL, BrainBody-LLM, InteractivePlanning, SelfRefine, incoro}. Nevertheless, challenges are prone. First, existing approaches prompt LLM with numerical state observations in scenarios that require insights into robot dynamics for evaluation~\cite{InteractivePlanning, incoro}. Given that LLMs exhibit limited numerical reasoning and processing capabilities~\cite{NumberUnderstanding}, an LLM-driven evaluation may overlook the latent semantics of robot actions and fail to achieve a comprehensive assessment of the robot's movements and dynamics, leading to unreliable code refinement feedback. Second, current closed-loop approaches require physical robot execution to generate state observations for evaluation~\cite{CoPAL, InteractivePlanning}. Such an approach may be acceptable for ground robots where occasional errors rarely cause irreparable damage. However, it poses risks for aerial platforms, as even slight control deviations can precipitate UAV crashes.

To address these challenges, we propose a novel closed-loop feedback and refinement framework that leverages semantic observations to enhance the reliability of LLM-driven UAV control as depicted in Fig. \ref{fig:overall}. Our framework comprises two LLM modules: a \textbf{Code Generator}, which synthesizes or refines UAV operation code based on task description or evaluation feedback; and an \textbf{Evaluator}, which assesses code performance and provides feedback to guide code refinement. The process unfolds as follows: 1) The code generator first produces an initial code based on the task description; 2) The code is then execute in simulation to produce raw numerical state observation, which are subsequently transformed into an NL trajectory observation; 3) The evaluator compares NL trajectory observation against the original task specification and returns evaluation feedback; 4) If the evaluation confirms the trajectory meets the task objectives, the code will deploy on the UAV for mission execution. Otherwise, the evaluation feedback is returned to the code generator for iterative refinement. By introducing NL trajectory representations rather than numerical state information, our method significantly enhances the evaluator's comprehension of the UAV's state dynamics, thereby improving the reliability and precision of the evaluation feedback. Moreover, our method enables the refinement process in simulation, which eliminates the risks to UAVs caused by incorrect code execution during refinement, and hence supports safe and efficient development.

We conduct extensive experiments on UAV operation tasks with varying levels of complexity to evaluate the effectiveness of our proposed framework. The experimental results show that our framework achieves a $100\%$ success rate and $100\%$ completeness for basic operations. For complex tasks, our framework achieves an $85\%$ success rate and $100\%$ completeness. Compared with baseline approaches \cite{GSCE,SelfRefine,InteractivePlanning}, our framework greatly improves both success rate and completeness when the task complexity increases. The contributions of our work can be summarized as follows:
\begin{itemize}
    \item \textbf{Closed-Loop Refinement:} A novel closed-loop feedback and refinement framework with a code generator and an evaluator designed to enhance the reliability of LLM-driven UAV control.
    \item \textbf{Semantic Encoding:} A novel algorithm for converting numerical state observations into step-wise NL trajectory descriptions, thereby enhancing the evaluative reasoning process.
    \item \textbf{Safe Development:} A simulation-based LLM-generated UAV code evaluation that replaces physical deployment to prevent potential UAV crashes in code refinement.
    \item \textbf{Comprehensive Evaluation}: A comprehensive experimental evaluation that demonstrates the performance of our framework with a detailed analysis of design factors. 
\end{itemize}
The remainder of this paper is structured as follows: Section \ref{s:related-work} reviews and discusses existing research. Section \ref{sec:problem formulation} presents our problem formulation, which is followed by the detailed design of our proposed framework in Section \ref{sec:method}. Section \ref{sec:evaluation} provides the evaluation and analysis of our framework. This paper is concluded in Section \ref{sec:conclusion}.


\section{Related Work}\label{s:related-work}

\subsection{LLM for Robotics}
LLMs have demonstrated exceptional capabilities in semantic understanding and context generation, making them invaluable in robotics. ChatGPT for Robotics~\cite{ChatGPTRobotics} and Code as Policies~\cite{CodeasPolicies} have shown significant progress in LLM to control various types of robots. In the domain of aerial platforms, TypeFly~\cite{TypeFly} introduces an end-to-end LLM framework for piloting drones via human languages, while REAL~\cite{REAL} integrates LLMs into a mission planning and control framework. Moreover, continual advancements in LLMs' reasoning and code generation capabilities~\cite{deepseek, o3mini} are opening new opportunities for a variety of LLM-driven robotic tasks. Despite these advances, robotic performance continues to be limited by the reasoning capacity of LLMs, which frequently demonstrate unreliability when faced with tasks requiring complex reasoning~\cite{ChatGPTRobotics}.

\subsection{Prompt Engineering}
Recent studies have increasingly employed prompt engineering~\cite{PromptEngineering} to improve the reliability of LLM-driven robotic systems. For instance, GSCE proposes a prompt framework as a system prompt to improve the reliability of LLM-driven UAV control code generation~\cite{GSCE}. Prompt engineering typically leverages two core techniques: in-context learning~\cite{URIAL} and Chain-of-Thought (CoT) reasoning~\cite{CoT}. In-context learning enables the LLM to identify patterns from a limited set of examples and apply them to new situations. This facilitates the generation of control code that adheres to robot policy and how to ground task descriptions from a few examples~\cite{CodeasPolicies, PromptBook}. On the other hand, CoT prompts the LLM to articulate intermediate inference steps, which is valuable for robotic tasks requiring sequential, stepwise decision-making. By decomposing a task into steps, CoT encourages the production of code that aligns with each stage of the intended action plan. Prior studies have embedded CoT within the examples to guide LLMs through reasoning step-by-step via in-context learning~\cite{ISR-LLM, PromptBook}. In this study, we integrate both CoT and in-context learning strategies to enhance the quality of code generation and evaluation in LLM-driven UAV control.

\subsection{LLM-based Feedback, Evaluation, and Refinement}
ChatGPT for Robotics emphasizes the necessity of human oversight for code generation~\cite{ChatGPTRobotics}, reflecting the fact that robotic performance remains constrained by LLMs' reasoning limitations. Moreover, LLMs exhibit sensitivity to input perturbations~\cite{JailbreakingLLM, PromptInjection, HighlightSafetyConcern}. Thus, recent research efforts adopt closed-loop refinement strategies to verify the LLM generation for improving the reliability of LLM-driven control. For instance, PluginSafety~\cite{PluginSafety} designs a module that enforces predefined logical constraints evaluation to ensure LLM-generated content adheres to predefined constraints. More recent studies employ corrective feedback to iteratively refine generated code. Some integrate human or automated checker feedback~\cite{AutoTAMP, ISR-LLM, Lifelong, EnsureSafety} to re-prompt the LLM and improve reasoning accuracy. While others monitor the robot status during execution and provide feedback, such as runtime errors, robot constraints violations~\cite{trust, CoPAL, BrainBody-LLM, InteractivePlanning}. The evaluation processes generally fall into two categories: self-evaluation and external evaluation. SELF-REFINE~\cite{SelfRefine, trust} leverages a single LLM to assess and refine its own outputs based on feedback, whereas external evaluation approaches employ a secondary LLM or external evaluator to generate corrective feedback~\cite{PluginSafety, CoPAL, BrainBody-LLM, InteractivePlanning}. In our approach, numerical state information is first transformed into NL observations, and then we use an external LLM-evaluator to provide feedback for iterative refinement.


