\subsection{Feedback and Refinement} \label{sec:feedback and refinement}
The feedback and refinement steer the LLM-generated UAV operation code towards fully satisfying the task objectives. They leverage the two LLM agents designed in section~\ref{sec:code generation} and section~\ref{sec:evaluation} and form a closed-loop as illustrated in Fig.~\ref{fig:feedback}. Specifically, the code generator interprets the evaluation feedback \(F\) and refines the generated code accordingly. Then, the refined code is executed in the simulation again to produce a new NL trajectory observation \(O'\) for re-evaluation. The feedback and refinement loop continues iteratively until the evaluator outputs "YES", indicating that \(E\) confirms that the refined action sequence \(l\) successfully solves the task problem \(P\) and the UAV operation code is ready for deployment. In addition, if the maximum number of iterations is reached without a successful evaluation, the evaluator suggests human intervention. 

%Overall, the UAV operation code is continuously improved through cycles of code generation, trajectory observation, evaluation, feedback, and refinement, ultimately leading to a reliable deployment-ready solution for UAV mission execution. 



\section{Experiment}
The goal of this experiment is to validate the effectiveness of our proposed LLM-driven closed-loop framework in enhancing the reliability of LLM-driven UAV operation code generation. To achieve this, the experiment is designed with three primary goals: (1) to evaluate the performance of our closed-loop feedback and refinement framework in LLM-driven UAV tasks; (2) to analyze the impact of different design choices on the performance of our framework; (3) to assess the behavior of our framework under diverse task scenarios.

\subsection{Experimental Setup}

\subsubsection{\textbf{Environment}}
To ensure safe code execution without risking real-world failures, we conduct experiments in the ``AirSim'' simulator \cite{AirSim}, which realistically simulates UAV (e.g., a quadcopter) dynamics and environmental interactions. During the experiment, UAV state information was fully accessible from the simulator in real time and utilized for closed-loop feedback and evaluation purposes.

\subsubsection{\textbf{Tasks}}
We propose \textbf{Basic} and \textbf{Advanced} task sets to rigorously evaluate the performance of LLM-driven control code generation across varying levels of operational complexity\footnote{The complete list of tasks is available at \url{https://github.com/ai-uavsec/CLGSCE}}. The \textbf{Basic} set comprises 44 tasks adopted from GSCE~\cite{GSCE}. Each task involves one or two intuitive discrete movements to evaluate LLM's few-stage planning and basic spatial reasoning (e.g., \textit{``Turn 90° clockwise, then fly 4 meters left in the drone's body frame''}). In contrast, the \textbf{Advanced} set consists of 20 tasks that emulate real-world UAV deployment (e.g., \textit{``Take off and fly up 5 meters. You should fly in a square pattern with 5-meter sides by moving forward, right, backward, and left. Make sure the drone is oriented to the flying direction.''}). These tasks demand reasoning about the objectives in the task description and require 6-19 UAV actions to reach the goal state. The advanced task set is intended to evaluate LLM's multi-stage planning and complex spatial reasoning. Additionally, all tasks in both task sets were manually validated to avoid potential simulator-induced errors that could affect the evaluation process.

