\subsubsection{\textbf{Compared Methods}} \label{sec:compared method}
Three recently proposed methods \cite{GSCE,SelfRefine,InteractivePlanning} are selected as the baselines for comparison with our approach, including 
\begin{itemize}
    \item \textbf{GSCE:} An open-loop approach in which the GSCE code generator synthesizes control code based on the task prompt, without any refinement.~\cite{GSCE}
          % \item \textbf{GSCE with extra examples (GSCE+):} This method augments the baseline GSCE by providing additional real-world exemplars in GSCE's examples component.
    \item \textbf{LLM Self-feedback (Self-Refine):} A single LLM performs both code generation and self-refinement. The LLM analyzes NL trajectory observations to detect deviations from the task and refines code based on its self-assessment. ~\cite{SelfRefine}
    \item \textbf{Feedback with Numerical State Observations (Numerical):} This method introduces a secondary LLM serving as evaluator designed according to section~\ref{sec:evaluation}. The evaluator detects deviations from numerical state observations and provides corrective guidance for the code generator for refinement.~\cite{InteractivePlanning}
    % \item \textbf{Our Approach (Ours):} Building on the Numerical approach, our method replaces raw numerical state observations with NL trajectory observation during the evaluation, as detailed in Algorithm~\ref{alg:overall}.
\end{itemize}

All methods described above employ ``o3-mini'' (o3-mini-2025-01-31)~\cite{o3mini} as the foundational LLM. The generated code was then deployed on a quadcopter in AirSim \cite{AirSim} to evaluate the performance of each method. To mitigate the randomness of LLM's output~\cite{LLMrandomness}, we repeat each task three times in the experiment.

\subsection{Evaluation Setup}
\subsubsection{\textbf{Metrics}}
The performance is evaluated using two metrics as GSCE~\cite{GSCE}: \textbf{Completeness} and \textbf{Success Rate (SR)}.

\textbf{Completeness} quantifies the proportion of goal states successfully reached, calculated as the ratio of the total count of correct actions to the total number of required actions of the ground truth trajectory in each task. The completeness offering insights into performance across the intermediate control process.

\begin{equation}
    Completeness = \frac{\sum\text{correct actions}}{\mathrm{len}(l)}
    \label{eq:completeness}
\end{equation}

\textbf{SR} is grounded in real-world operational requirements, wherein the UAV must fly the trajectory precisely as humans instructed. As formalized in Eq.~\ref{eq:success}, a task is counted as a success only when the UAV reaches the final state while following the correct sequence of actions that lead to the ground truth trajectory.
\begin{equation}
    Success =
    \begin{cases}
        1, & Completeness = 1,    \\
        0, & Completeness < 1.
    \end{cases}
    \label{eq:success}
\end{equation}

\subsubsection{\textbf{Ground Truth}}
The ground truth is represented by a list of state transitions. It is a vector of four elements: \([x, y, z, \theta]\), where \(x\), \(y\), and \(z\) denote the UAV's position changes in the North, East, and Down axes, and \(\theta\) represents yaw rotation. We assume the UAV's state is fully observable at all times, thereby ensuring that each transition can be precisely monitored and compared against ground truth.


\subsection{Result and Analysis}

\subsubsection{\textbf{Overall Result}}

The average SR and completeness across Basic and Advanced tasks are summarized in Table~\ref{tab:overall results}. For the Basic tasks, all methods showed remarkable performance. Specifically, the GSCE, Self-Refine, and Ours methods achieved $100\%$ in both SR and completeness, while the Numerical achieved $95.5\%$ SR and $97.3\%$ completeness. This demonstrates their ability to generate correct code for a few-stage planning and basic spatial reasoning. However, the slightly lower performance of the Numerical method suggests that relying on numerical state observations can occasionally lead to misinterpretation in evaluation, resulting in incomplete or incorrect refinements.

Performance on the Advanced tasks declines notably across all methods as a result of increased task complexity. The open-loop GSCE method achieves only $66.7\%$ SR, indicating that refinement becomes critical as task complexity increases. The Self-Refine method performs worse, with $50.0\%$ SR and $74.3\%$ completeness, proving that relying on a single LLM for both generation and self-evaluation amplifies bias~\cite{LLM-bias}, resulting in introducing additional errors in refinement. The Numerical method achieves $75\%$ SR and $92.4\%$ completeness, showing that it can detect some deviations; however, its numeric perspective still fails to provide reliable evaluation feedback for refinement. In contrast, our proposed method substantially outperforms all baselines, achieving $90.0\%$ SR and $98.3\%$ completeness on Advanced tasks. The overall results demonstrate that our proposed framework significantly enhances the reliability of LLM-driven UAV operation code generation, particularly for complex tasks that require multi-stage planning and complex spatial reasoning.

