\subsubsection{\textbf{Failure Cases Analysis}}
We further analyze the failure cases (cases that $Success = 0$) in the Advanced task set. The analysis focuses on three aspects: (1) the number of failures and average completeness for each failed task ID with the respective ground truth action length (Fig.~\ref{fig:failed count}); (2) the average completeness of each failed task ID (Fig.~\ref{fig:failed completeness}); (3) the amount of task that experienced failure and the average completeness of all failed tasks (Table~\ref{tab:failed tasks count and completeness}).

The results in Fig.~\ref{fig:failed count} reveal a clear trend that all methods exhibit a notable increase in failures as task complexity increases (i.e., more likely to fail on tasks with longer action length). Specifically, the baseline GSCE method failed in $11$ out of $20$ tasks ($55\%$ of the Advanced set), with an average completeness of $77.4\%$. This indicates that the GSCE method can reason through a substantial portion of the task correctly, however, it still struggles to maintain full alignment with the objectives of the task. This result highlights the need for improvement in the completeness of failed tasks to reduce overall failure rates. The Self-Refine method exhibited the weakest performance, with failures on $17$ tasks ($85\%$ of Advanced tasks). Moreover, the failures in Self-Refine were the least complete among all methods, demonstrating that performing evaluation and refinement on a single LLM not only fails to correct errors but may also introduce more errors. The Numerical method failed on $9$ tasks ($45\%$ of Advanced tasks) and achieved an average completeness of $69.5\%$ only. These results indicate that numerical state observations are insufficient for reliable code refinement and may even lead to erroneous refinement.


