\subsubsection{\textbf{Number of Refinement Iterations}}
We further investigate how SR and completeness evolve as the number of refinement iterations increases. As shown in Fig.~\ref{fig:number of itr}, the x-axis represents the iteration count from $0$ to $10$ ($0$ denotes no refinement, which is equivalent to the GSCE baseline). Both metrics show substantial improvement after the first iteration. Although a slight decline is observed in the second iteration, the performance gradually improves in subsequent iterations. Optimal performance is achieved in the $6\textsuperscript{th}$ iterations ($85\%$ SR, $98.5\%$ completeness). This demonstrates that iterative refinement effectively enhances the reliability of LLM-driven UAV operation by the code generator progressively correcting errors according to evaluation feedback. Furthermore, performance slightly declines beyond $6$ iterations, implying that excessive refinement can lead to diminishing returns or even introduce new errors. This observation highlights the necessity of imposing an upper bound on refinement iterations to avoid overfitting or introducing instability in the code refinement process. In our method, we recommend setting the maximum number of iterations to $6$.

