\subsection{Evaluator Configuration Analysis}
As the precision of the evaluation feedback directly influences the quality of code refinement, we also analyzed how different evaluator configurations affect evaluation precision. The evaluation precision is defined as the ratio of correct evaluations (i.e., correctly identifying whether the trajectory aligns with the task and providing a detailed explanation if deviations are identified) to the total number of evaluations conducted. To perform this analysis, we built a dataset from each Advanced task with one correct NL trajectory observation (Correct O) and one incorrect NL trajectory observation (Incorrect O). As presented in Table~\ref{tab:evaluator configuration}, we tested four configurations of the evaluator system prompt: (1) roles; (2) roles $+$ rules; (3) roles $+$ references; (4) roles $+$ rules $+$ references. Moreover, each configuration was evaluated three times to reduce the randomness of LLM's output~\cite{LLMrandomness}.


