The alphabet theory is assumed to be drawn from an effective Boolean algebra, defined below. 
\paragraph{Boolean Algebra} 
An effective Boolean algebra $\mathcal{A}$ is a tuple $(\domain, \Psi, \denotations{-}, \bot, \top, \lor, \land, \neg)$ where $\domain$ is a set of domain elements; $\Psi$ is a set of predicates closed under the Boolean connectives with $\top, \bot \in \Psi$; $\denotations{-} : \Psi \rightarrow 2^{\mathfrak{D}}$ is a denotation function such that 
\begin{inparaenum}[(i)]
	\item 
	$\denotations{\bot} = \emptyset$, 
	\item 
	$\denotations{\top} = \domain$, and 
	\item 
	for all $\varphi, \psi \in \Psi$, 
	$\denotations{\varphi \lor \psi} = \denotations{\varphi} \cup \denotations{\psi}$,
	$\denotations{\varphi \land \psi} = \denotations{\varphi} \cap \denotations{\psi}$, and 
	$\denotations{\neg \varphi} = \domain \setminus \denotations{\varphi}$. 
\end{inparaenum}
We require that checking satisfiability of $\varphi$, i.e. whether $\denotations{\varphi} \neq \emptyset$, is decidable.
