% Large Language Models (LLMs) are increasingly deployed in sensitive domains such as healthcare, finance, and legal services, where the risk of inadvertently leaking private information during inference is a growing concern. Despite significant progress in privacy extraction attacks and privacy protection techniques, there is a notable gap in methods specifically designed for privacy detection during the inference phase. Existing approaches focus primarily on privacy extraction, such as jailbreaking and membership inference attacks, but do not directly address the challenge of detecting private information in model outputs. In this paper, we introduce PrivacyXray, a novel approach for detecting privacy breaches in LLM outputs by analyzing the internal states of the model. Inspired by X-ray imaging, PrivacyXray diagnoses potential privacy leaks by capturing and evaluating key features of the model's inner states, including semantic coherence and probabilistic certainty, without requiring access to external data or sensitive training data. We address the challenge of privacy detection by synthesizing private data for fine-tuning and proposing four key metrics: intra-layer and inter-layer semantic similarity, as well as layer-level and sentence-level probability distributions. These metrics form the basis of a highly accurate, efficient, and generalizable privacy detection framework.Our experiments show that PrivacyXray achieves high detection accuracy (85.4\% to 92.44\%) with limited training data (as few as 4,500 samples). Furthermore, it demonstrates strong data generalization and model transferability, maintaining high accuracy across different domains and LLM architectures. To our knowledge, PrivacyXray is the first method to offer a practical and scalable solution for privacy detection during LLM inference, capable of being applied in real-world, privacy-sensitive applications.

